WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:13.513
[clattering]

2
00:00:16.891 --> 00:00:18.101
[clattering]

3
00:00:31.239 --> 00:00:32.699
[Alexandria] Getting ready,

4
00:00:34.159 --> 00:00:37.412
for women, it involves so many decisions

5
00:00:37.495 --> 00:00:41.082
about how you're gonna present yourself
to the world.

6
00:00:43.001 --> 00:00:45.378
'Cause there's kind of standard protocol

7
00:00:45.462 --> 00:00:48.757
for how a man running for office, like,

8
00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:50.341
should dress.

9
00:00:50.425 --> 00:00:52.010
You either put on a suit,

10
00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:56.723
or you put on a light color shirt, slacks,
and you roll up the sleeves.

11
00:00:56.806 --> 00:00:57.849
Or...

12
00:00:57.932 --> 00:01:00.226
I mean, those are pretty much
your two options.

13
00:01:02.437 --> 00:01:04.939
Part of me is,
like, trying to brace myself

14
00:01:06.024 --> 00:01:08.193
because people keep telling me,

15
00:01:08.276 --> 00:01:10.153
"They're gonna try
to make sure that you...

16
00:01:11.362 --> 00:01:15.700
just never show your face
in your community again."

17
00:01:17.577 --> 00:01:20.163
How do you prepare for something
you don't know is coming?

18
00:01:21.456 --> 00:01:22.874
[whimsical music playing]

19
00:01:44.729 --> 00:01:47.023
Hey, everyone,
I'm getting ready to go file today.

20
00:01:47.107 --> 00:01:48.358
Super excited.

21
00:01:48.441 --> 00:01:50.819
It's official. We are in the race.

22
00:01:51.277 --> 00:01:52.445
Go, Team Vilela.

23
00:02:00.537 --> 00:02:03.623
Hey, everyone, it's Cori Bush,
candidate for United States Congress,

24
00:02:03.706 --> 00:02:05.041
here in Missouri's first district.

25
00:02:05.125 --> 00:02:07.335
[man] Hello, everybody,
this is Anthony Vincent Clark.

26
00:02:07.418 --> 00:02:10.296
- [woman 1] Hi, it's Rashida Tlaib.
- [woman 2] Second district of Nebraska...

27
00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:11.881
[overlapping dialogue]

28
00:02:30.859 --> 00:02:34.529
[Paula] We shouldn't have to worry
about self-serving politicians

29
00:02:34.946 --> 00:02:36.281
catering to coal barons

30
00:02:36.364 --> 00:02:39.367
instead of catering to our families
and communities.

31
00:02:39.742 --> 00:02:43.997
I plan to Primary Joe Manchin in 2018,

32
00:02:44.622 --> 00:02:47.041
and I'm asking you to stand with me.

33
00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:49.419
[female voice fades in]

34
00:02:49.502 --> 00:02:53.840
...with some of these incumbent Democrats
who are facing primary challenges.

35
00:02:53.923 --> 00:02:57.427
[man] But are the progressives going to
make the Democrat primaries messy...

36
00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:58.469
[voice fades out]

37
00:03:04.976 --> 00:03:06.477
[Alexandria] We're running to organize.

38
00:03:06.811 --> 00:03:10.273
We're running to redefine
the political landscape in New York City.

39
00:03:11.316 --> 00:03:13.151
And here's the best part
about all of this:

40
00:03:13.234 --> 00:03:14.861
We're not running to make a statement.

41
00:03:14.944 --> 00:03:17.447
We're not running to pressure
the incumbent to the left.

42
00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:18.740
We're running to win.

43
00:03:26.206 --> 00:03:27.248
[music stops]

44
00:03:38.426 --> 00:03:40.011
[Alexandria] Morning, guys. Buenos días.

45
00:03:40.511 --> 00:03:42.013
- [men] Hola.
- [in Spanish] How are you?

46
00:03:54.359 --> 00:03:55.818
[elevator beeps]

47
00:03:58.655 --> 00:04:01.407
If I was, like, a normal, rational person,

48
00:04:01.491 --> 00:04:04.661
I would've dropped out of this race
a long time ago. [chuckles]

49
00:04:12.794 --> 00:04:16.923
[Alexandria] People don't see waitresses
as having a, quote-unquote, "real job."

50
00:04:18.591 --> 00:04:21.219
But my experience in hospitality

51
00:04:21.302 --> 00:04:24.013
has prepared me so well for this race.

52
00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:28.142
I'm used to being on my feet
18 hours a day,

53
00:04:28.226 --> 00:04:32.105
I'm used to receiving a lot of heat.

54
00:04:32.522 --> 00:04:35.775
I'm used to people
trying to make me feel bad.

55
00:04:38.027 --> 00:04:40.446
They call it "working class" for a reason.

56
00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:43.783
Because you are working non-stop.

57
00:04:49.289 --> 00:04:51.791
Americans aren't asking for a lot.

58
00:04:51.874 --> 00:04:54.377
They're just asking to get by.

59
00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:58.923
And they're just asking for politicians
to be brave enough to help them get by.

60
00:05:02.093 --> 00:05:02.927
[cork pops]

61
00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:07.432
[male voice] This is WNYC FM, New York.

62
00:05:08.975 --> 00:05:10.893
Every political machine has a leader

63
00:05:10.977 --> 00:05:14.397
and the most powerful of them
are known everywhere as the boss.

64
00:05:14.814 --> 00:05:16.899
In Queens, that's Congressman Joe Crowley,

65
00:05:16.983 --> 00:05:19.193
Chairman of the County
Democratic Committee

66
00:05:19.277 --> 00:05:21.321
and also the Chairman
of the House Democratic Caucus,

67
00:05:21.779 --> 00:05:24.991
making him the fourth most powerful
Democrat in Congress.

68
00:05:25.074 --> 00:05:29.495
What we're talking about is better jobs,
better pay and a better future.

69
00:05:29.579 --> 00:05:33.082
[female voice] Crowley hasn't had
a primary challenger in 14 years,

70
00:05:33.458 --> 00:05:34.625
until now.

71
00:05:43.176 --> 00:05:46.012
[Alexandria] I just want to thank
everyone here so much.

72
00:05:46.095 --> 00:05:47.847
I mean, this is the work.

73
00:05:47.930 --> 00:05:51.893
What I do is just talk to people
all the time.

74
00:05:51.976 --> 00:05:54.354
Just members of our community,
all the time.

75
00:05:54.437 --> 00:05:56.647
And that is the organizing
and that is the work

76
00:05:56.731 --> 00:05:59.025
of running a grassroots campaign.

77
00:05:59.442 --> 00:06:02.195
This is not just about
Democrat versus Republican,

78
00:06:02.278 --> 00:06:04.364
in fact, it's so far away from that.

79
00:06:04.447 --> 00:06:07.241
It's not Left or Right, it's up and down.

80
00:06:07.742 --> 00:06:12.747
[Alexandria] We're up against an opponent,
he's taken three million dollars per cycle

81
00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:17.043
from Wall Street, real estate,
and pharmaceutical companies.

82
00:06:17.126 --> 00:06:21.631
We need to have the courage
to stand up for working people

83
00:06:21.714 --> 00:06:24.967
and stand up against corporate interests.

84
00:06:25.051 --> 00:06:27.887
[man]
Let's assume all the energy in this room

85
00:06:27.970 --> 00:06:31.599
can get you on the ballot
and into office.

86
00:06:31.682 --> 00:06:35.645
How then do we overcome the drop in power?

87
00:06:35.728 --> 00:06:39.690
We're trading a guy who's vying
for the Speaker of the House,

88
00:06:40.358 --> 00:06:45.363
for somebody who's more in our corner
and has a lot less power.

89
00:06:45.446 --> 00:06:47.573
[Alexandria]
I think we really need to look at...

90
00:06:47.990 --> 00:06:50.451
what that power does now.

91
00:06:50.535 --> 00:06:53.037
When it matters,
he doesn't stand up for us.

92
00:06:53.121 --> 00:06:55.873
When it matters,
he doesn't advocate for our interests.

93
00:06:56.249 --> 00:06:58.960
We have to have the courage to say,
"We can do better."

94
00:06:59.043 --> 00:07:02.088
We can do better.
It's not gonna be a loss.

95
00:07:04.632 --> 00:07:05.842
[soft electronic music playing]

96
00:07:20.898 --> 00:07:22.567
[man 1] What would you reckon the...

97
00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:24.902
evidence of selflessness is?

98
00:07:25.027 --> 00:07:27.447
[man 2] She is pretty doggone stellar.

99
00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:29.365
I talked to her today.

100
00:07:29.949 --> 00:07:32.577
Has not thought of running before.

101
00:07:32.994 --> 00:07:35.329
I talked about the economic platform,
she loved it.

102
00:07:35.413 --> 00:07:38.082
There wasn't a piece of the platform
she wasn't onboard with 100%.

103
00:07:38.166 --> 00:07:41.377
[woman] All those in favor of moving her
to the next round, raise your hand.

104
00:07:43.045 --> 00:07:45.256
[man] At the first time,
we've gotten 100%?

105
00:07:45.339 --> 00:07:47.383
I think that might have been
the first time!

106
00:07:47.467 --> 00:07:48.634
[instrumental music playing]

107
00:07:59.854 --> 00:08:01.856
[man] For Brand New Congress
and Justice Democrats,

108
00:08:01.939 --> 00:08:04.317
the biggest shared goal is removing

109
00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:06.861
the corrupting influence
of money in politics.

110
00:08:06.986 --> 00:08:08.112
[indistinct chatter]

111
00:08:08.321 --> 00:08:11.449
[Corbin] The idea is to provide
an alternative path to Congress

112
00:08:11.532 --> 00:08:13.284
outside of the current one that exists.

113
00:08:13.743 --> 00:08:17.538
Right now we have a path through lobbyists
and through special interest groups.

114
00:08:17.622 --> 00:08:19.957
Right now our Congress is 81% men,

115
00:08:20.041 --> 00:08:22.835
it's mostly white men,
it's mostly millionaires,

116
00:08:22.919 --> 00:08:24.545
it's mostly lawyers.

117
00:08:25.171 --> 00:08:27.423
[man] Most people in there
are just trying to figure out

118
00:08:27.507 --> 00:08:29.425
how to keep their job
and win their next election.

119
00:08:29.634 --> 00:08:32.053
That's not the thinking
that's going to fix climate change,

120
00:08:32.136 --> 00:08:33.471
or mass incarceration,

121
00:08:33.554 --> 00:08:35.431
or these giant problems
that are facing us.

122
00:08:35.515 --> 00:08:37.475
The only way this works is if--

123
00:08:37.558 --> 00:08:40.102
is if we really capture
America's imagination.

124
00:08:40.186 --> 00:08:41.979
[woman 1]
We're looking at Democrats and Republicans

125
00:08:42.063 --> 00:08:44.148
to run in the midterms in 2018.

126
00:08:44.232 --> 00:08:45.983
[woman 2] Non-career politicians.

127
00:08:46.067 --> 00:08:48.778
So, nurses, teachers,
engineers, workers...

128
00:08:48.861 --> 00:08:50.279
[woman 3] We don't care about party.

129
00:08:50.780 --> 00:08:52.406
We just want to get stuff done.

130
00:08:52.823 --> 00:08:54.659
If we elect working people,

131
00:08:54.742 --> 00:08:57.161
working people
can have representation in Congress.

132
00:08:57.245 --> 00:08:59.455
We can change the way we see government,

133
00:08:59.539 --> 00:09:01.791
change the way we see politics
in this country.

134
00:09:14.804 --> 00:09:15.805
[music fades out]

135
00:09:16.639 --> 00:09:18.474
[Saikat]
So, I'm so glad all of y'all are here.

136
00:09:18.933 --> 00:09:21.310
This--
This whole thing is making me feel like

137
00:09:21.519 --> 00:09:24.689
there's no way that we're gonna go stop
this snowball that's now getting rolling.

138
00:09:24.772 --> 00:09:26.899
It's going to become an avalanche
in a couple of months.

139
00:09:27.483 --> 00:09:30.528
[Saikat] We want to actually create
that grassroots-funded machine

140
00:09:30.611 --> 00:09:33.823
that can be your real opposition
to the current institutional powers

141
00:09:33.906 --> 00:09:36.701
and that can really give power
back to the people

142
00:09:36.784 --> 00:09:38.995
and really keep that power. Right?

143
00:09:39.954 --> 00:09:41.622
[Alexandria] I felt like there was...

144
00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:44.709
such a wide divide in this nation

145
00:09:44.792 --> 00:09:47.086
in terms of how we understand each other,

146
00:09:47.169 --> 00:09:48.963
how we speak to one another,

147
00:09:49.046 --> 00:09:51.632
and, in December,

148
00:09:51.716 --> 00:09:53.009
I get a call from Isra saying,

149
00:09:53.092 --> 00:09:55.219
"You've been nominated
to Brand New Congress."

150
00:09:55.595 --> 00:09:57.513
And I was like, "What?"

151
00:09:57.597 --> 00:09:58.848
[all laughing]

152
00:09:59.015 --> 00:10:00.349
[Alexandria] You know, I'm from New York,

153
00:10:00.433 --> 00:10:02.893
and New York isn't Democrat
versus Republican.

154
00:10:02.977 --> 00:10:04.729
New York is establishment

155
00:10:04.812 --> 00:10:08.941
versus whatever poor, stray cat
thinks you can stand up against them.

156
00:10:09.025 --> 00:10:10.901
And then if you do stand up against them,

157
00:10:10.985 --> 00:10:13.195
they take you and they consume you.

158
00:10:13.571 --> 00:10:16.240
I never really saw myself
going into politics.

159
00:10:16.324 --> 00:10:19.285
But now that I sit here in this room
with you all, I just feel like...

160
00:10:19.577 --> 00:10:21.662
we can only accomplish
great things together.

161
00:10:22.413 --> 00:10:25.458
I feel like I have to
because of you guys now.

162
00:10:25.541 --> 00:10:27.209
[all laughing]

163
00:10:27.793 --> 00:10:31.047
You have to be fearless,
'cause they're going to come after you.

164
00:10:32.298 --> 00:10:35.176
They are, this is no joke, y'all.
They're going to come after you.

165
00:10:36.427 --> 00:10:37.803
But you have to be fearless.

166
00:10:39.430 --> 00:10:40.848
Nobody owns you yet.

167
00:10:42.350 --> 00:10:44.477
You don't owe anybody anything yet.

168
00:10:44.935 --> 00:10:45.770
So, run.

169
00:10:46.187 --> 00:10:49.523
You got people who are waiting for y'all
to come out this room.

170
00:10:50.524 --> 00:10:52.985
To come back to Florida, and to Georgia,

171
00:10:53.069 --> 00:10:55.196
and to Alabama, and to New York.

172
00:10:55.613 --> 00:10:57.448
They want to be excited again.

173
00:10:58.491 --> 00:10:59.909
Our future is in this room.

174
00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:02.328
[distant siren wailing]

175
00:11:12.046 --> 00:11:13.881
- I'm nervous.
- [woman laughs]

176
00:11:14.382 --> 00:11:15.257
[doorbell rings]

177
00:11:17.176 --> 00:11:19.970
[Alexandria whispers] What do you say?
When they say, "Who is it?"

178
00:11:20.262 --> 00:11:21.347
What do you say?

179
00:11:22.264 --> 00:11:23.099
[woman] Hello?

180
00:11:23.182 --> 00:11:25.142
Hi, my name is Mathew,
I'm out here canvassing

181
00:11:25.226 --> 00:11:28.437
for a Congressional candidate
named Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez,

182
00:11:28.521 --> 00:11:30.189
for the Democratic Party in 2018.

183
00:11:30.272 --> 00:11:32.316
I was wondering
if I could talk to you for a minute.

184
00:11:32.817 --> 00:11:34.485
[woman] No, I'm sorry.
Now is not a good time.

185
00:11:34.568 --> 00:11:36.570
- Okay, thank you.
- Thank you.

186
00:11:36.904 --> 00:11:37.738
Easy.

187
00:11:37.822 --> 00:11:38.781
[doorbell rings]

188
00:11:39.699 --> 00:11:41.158
[woman] Me no speak English.

189
00:11:41.242 --> 00:11:43.494
- [Alexandria] Oh, what do you speak?
- [woman] Greek, Greek.

190
00:11:43.577 --> 00:11:44.620
Oh, Greek?

191
00:11:44.745 --> 00:11:48.374
[man] Why do you let people in the country
even though they're not legal--?

192
00:11:48.457 --> 00:11:51.085
You know, they say,
"Okay, we're gonna forgive you."

193
00:11:51.168 --> 00:11:53.087
- [overlapping dialogue]
- I don't agree with that.

194
00:11:53.170 --> 00:11:55.214
I'm a Democrat,
and I don't even agree with that.

195
00:11:55.297 --> 00:11:56.173
Well, I think--

196
00:11:56.257 --> 00:11:59.301
I voted for Trump, and I am a Democrat.
And she did too.

197
00:12:03.097 --> 00:12:05.850
[Alexandria] It's so crazy,
'cause speaking to groups of people...

198
00:12:05.933 --> 00:12:08.060
- [man] Right, it's easy.
- [Alexandria] It's way easier.

199
00:12:09.311 --> 00:12:10.438
[doorbell rings]

200
00:12:10.604 --> 00:12:11.814
[emotional music playing]

201
00:12:14.567 --> 00:12:15.818
[Alexandria] In the beginning,

202
00:12:16.527 --> 00:12:19.655
the fundamental question is like,
"Why you?"

203
00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:23.826
"Why do you think you can do this?"

204
00:12:25.870 --> 00:12:27.413
The reason "why"

205
00:12:27.496 --> 00:12:30.082
was 'cause nobody else would.

206
00:12:31.292 --> 00:12:35.671
So, literally anybody could, right?

207
00:12:36.005 --> 00:12:36.839
[music fades out]

208
00:12:37.298 --> 00:12:39.341
Because the alternative is no one.

209
00:12:39.425 --> 00:12:40.509
[music resumes]

210
00:12:46.348 --> 00:12:49.935
[Alexandria] We have never seen this,
at this scale, in American history.

211
00:12:50.019 --> 00:12:52.396
We have slates and legions of candidates

212
00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:54.857
that are running now
and in 2018

213
00:12:54.940 --> 00:12:57.401
that have rejected corporate funds.

214
00:12:57.860 --> 00:13:01.030
Many of us, you know, we're doing
something very "controversial."

215
00:13:01.697 --> 00:13:04.200
We're daring to actually force
the primary.

216
00:13:04.283 --> 00:13:06.243
People would say,
"How dare you mount a challenge

217
00:13:06.327 --> 00:13:08.037
to someone who's so established?"

218
00:13:08.120 --> 00:13:10.331
Well, if they're good enough, they'll win.

219
00:13:10.414 --> 00:13:12.249
And if we're good enough, we'll win.

220
00:13:12.333 --> 00:13:13.793
[distant indistinct chatter]

221
00:13:14.126 --> 00:13:16.253
[Paula] She said,
"You shouldn't show your emotion,

222
00:13:16.337 --> 00:13:19.256
because women are considered fragile
if you do.

223
00:13:19.340 --> 00:13:21.258
And you need to be more of a bitch."

224
00:13:21.675 --> 00:13:24.053
[man] That is the worst advice
anyone can fucking give.

225
00:13:24.136 --> 00:13:26.597
[Paula] He said, "And when you go
to the people in West Virginia

226
00:13:26.680 --> 00:13:28.474
and you tell 'em you're serving them,"
he says,

227
00:13:28.557 --> 00:13:30.392
"You need to tell them
that you're their bitch."

228
00:13:30.476 --> 00:13:32.436
- I said, "I'm not a dog."
- [man] No! That's horri--

229
00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:34.188
- That's such horrible advice!
- [all laughing]

230
00:13:34.271 --> 00:13:37.024
We work harder in these races
just 'cause we're women.

231
00:13:37.107 --> 00:13:39.235
- We're not rich, white dudes in suits.
- [man] Absolutely.

232
00:13:39.568 --> 00:13:41.612
[indistinct chatter]

233
00:13:55.626 --> 00:13:56.669
[piano music playing]

234
00:13:56.752 --> 00:13:57.962
I don't know where I'm going.

235
00:13:59.922 --> 00:14:01.799
- [man] How are you feeling?
- Nervous.

236
00:14:03.050 --> 00:14:04.301
[Paula] Was this a good ending?

237
00:14:04.802 --> 00:14:05.803
Let me read those.

238
00:14:06.262 --> 00:14:09.265
Okay, so I'm taking these away from you
for a second.

239
00:14:09.807 --> 00:14:10.683
Why?

240
00:14:10.766 --> 00:14:15.646
Words don't matter so much right now
as you, right?

241
00:14:16.021 --> 00:14:20.484
You're up there to convey your emotion
and to inspire people.

242
00:14:20.568 --> 00:14:24.071
The words are useful in that,
but that's not the most important thing.

243
00:14:24.363 --> 00:14:26.073
