you have to dress like this.

487
00:28:29.999 --> 00:28:33.044
I decided that, yeah, I don't care.

488
00:28:33.795 --> 00:28:35.630
- [man] One, two, three.
- [camera shutter clicks]

489
00:28:35.755 --> 00:28:37.298
[Cori] Basically you deal with it.

490
00:28:37.382 --> 00:28:39.759
You know, people in my district,
this is how we look.

491
00:28:40.385 --> 00:28:43.805
I'm going to serve and represent
the people of my district.

492
00:28:46.766 --> 00:28:48.852
[man] Anybody can run,
but you're doing a walk-through.

493
00:28:48.935 --> 00:28:51.271
- Yeah. A walk-through.
- That's why I'm running.

494
00:28:51.354 --> 00:28:55.150
Because this shouldn't be about one person
being a king, being like, "Kiss my ring."

495
00:28:55.233 --> 00:28:56.484
It's not about that.

496
00:28:56.735 --> 00:28:58.653
- Because we don't get help that way.
- [man] You're right.

497
00:28:58.737 --> 00:29:00.280
It's gotta be about us.

498
00:29:00.363 --> 00:29:02.282
It's gotta be about us thinking about us,

499
00:29:02.365 --> 00:29:04.284
caring about us, speaking about us.

500
00:29:04.367 --> 00:29:05.952
- Mm-hmm.
- [Cori] Not being afraid.

501
00:29:06.035 --> 00:29:07.454
Yeah. Looking for a real change.

502
00:29:07.537 --> 00:29:08.663
Looking for some real change.

503
00:29:08.747 --> 00:29:10.081
- [man] Not promises.
- [Cori] Oh, no.

504
00:29:10.165 --> 00:29:11.958
- Action.
- Action. Action.

505
00:29:25.638 --> 00:29:26.514
[music fades out]

506
00:29:26.598 --> 00:29:29.517
[man] St. Louis, in Missouri's
first Congressional district,

507
00:29:29.601 --> 00:29:32.896
had been represented
by William or his son Lacy Clay

508
00:29:32.979 --> 00:29:34.689
since 1969.

509
00:29:35.023 --> 00:29:37.358
If you look at the progress
that has been made

510
00:29:37.442 --> 00:29:41.196
over the past 50 or 60 years,
it's been remarkable.

511
00:29:41.738 --> 00:29:43.782
[man] So, you're running against, uh...

512
00:29:43.865 --> 00:29:44.741
[Cori] Clay.

513
00:29:44.824 --> 00:29:45.658
Okay.

514
00:29:47.160 --> 00:29:48.286
- How you doing?
- I'm good.

515
00:29:48.369 --> 00:29:51.873
Well, you have to forgive,
'cause it's just out of habit on--

516
00:29:52.123 --> 00:29:54.125
on the Clay family

517
00:29:54.876 --> 00:29:56.753
- from way, way back.
- Yeah.

518
00:29:56.836 --> 00:29:58.546
I know my pop was saying,

519
00:29:59.798 --> 00:30:00.632
"Who?"

520
00:30:00.715 --> 00:30:01.549
[Cori chuckles]

521
00:30:01.925 --> 00:30:05.970
[man] But, of course, the whole point,
you get seniority in that place up there,

522
00:30:06.054 --> 00:30:07.222
it does count.

523
00:30:07.555 --> 00:30:09.891
[Cori] Yeah.
It-- But who does it count for?

524
00:30:09.974 --> 00:30:13.853
Is it definitely counting for us?
Do we feel the seniority?

525
00:30:15.146 --> 00:30:18.691
I admire your guts
because this is an uphill battle.

526
00:30:19.108 --> 00:30:20.443
Because it's almost odd--

527
00:30:20.527 --> 00:30:21.820
You know, it's not even a thought

528
00:30:21.903 --> 00:30:24.239
for most of the folks
that I know that vote.

529
00:30:24.656 --> 00:30:27.450
Election time come round. "Okay. Clay."

530
00:30:27.534 --> 00:30:29.035
That's one of the few ones--

531
00:30:29.118 --> 00:30:32.997
Nobody even bother
looking at anything else.

532
00:30:33.081 --> 00:30:36.167
I ain't really seen no Democrat
with the guts

533
00:30:36.251 --> 00:30:38.753
to go up against the Clays in a long time.

534
00:30:43.424 --> 00:30:45.468
[man] Ladies and gentlemen,
welcome to 950 Lounge

535
00:30:45.552 --> 00:30:48.555
Miss Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez.
- Thank you.

536
00:30:48.638 --> 00:30:50.598
- Welcome to the show.
- Thank you, guys, so much.

537
00:30:50.682 --> 00:30:55.311
[Alexandria] So New York City
and New York politics is wild!

538
00:30:55.395 --> 00:30:58.815
We're so used to seeing
our elected officials in handcuffs

539
00:30:58.898 --> 00:31:01.651
- that it's like no big deal anymore.
- [all laughing]

540
00:31:01.734 --> 00:31:03.778
Our current Congressman, Joseph Crowley,

541
00:31:03.862 --> 00:31:05.446
never even won the seat.

542
00:31:05.530 --> 00:31:09.075
His mentor used a loophole
in New York City election law

543
00:31:09.158 --> 00:31:12.745
- to appoint him as the Democratic nominee.
- Yes.

544
00:31:12.829 --> 00:31:13.663
[man] Wow.

545
00:31:13.746 --> 00:31:15.248
He doesn't even live here.

546
00:31:15.331 --> 00:31:18.293
- He lives in Virginia, y'all.
- What!

547
00:31:18.376 --> 00:31:20.670
- Yes.
- All right, so hold on. You got a--

548
00:31:21.170 --> 00:31:23.590
- I'm assuming he's a Caucasian man.
- Yes, he is.

549
00:31:23.673 --> 00:31:25.174
You got a Caucasian man

550
00:31:25.258 --> 00:31:29.470
that's a Congressman for the Bronx,
Queens, and Rikers Island

551
00:31:29.554 --> 00:31:30.638
that lives in Virginia.

552
00:31:30.722 --> 00:31:32.682
It just don't make no common sense
to anybody.

553
00:31:32.765 --> 00:31:33.933
I don't know what y'all doing.

554
00:31:34.017 --> 00:31:35.894
If you popped him in the Bronx,
he would get lost.

555
00:31:40.440 --> 00:31:43.818
[woman]
I am so happy that I get to be here today

556
00:31:43.902 --> 00:31:46.821
as a surrogate for Congressman Crowley.

557
00:31:46.905 --> 00:31:51.409
He is extremely saddened
he couldn't be here with us today,

558
00:31:51.492 --> 00:31:55.121
and it's not
because he did not want to be here,

559
00:31:55.204 --> 00:31:59.584
and because he doesn't believe
that these types of forums are important.

560
00:31:59.667 --> 00:32:03.004
[Alexandria] We are making excuses
for absentee leadership.

561
00:32:03.087 --> 00:32:04.339
This is not a debate!

562
00:32:04.422 --> 00:32:06.424
[crowd cheering and applauding]

563
00:32:06.674 --> 00:32:10.553
With all respect--
With all respect to Councilwoman Palma,

564
00:32:10.637 --> 00:32:13.640
I thank you for your service
to our community. I do.

565
00:32:14.140 --> 00:32:16.851
But I am the only one
running for Congress in this room.

566
00:32:17.018 --> 00:32:19.729
[crowd cheering and applauding]

567
00:32:19.812 --> 00:32:23.900
[moderator] Question is about trusting
Congressman Crowley's judgment

568
00:32:23.983 --> 00:32:26.235
when he votes for the Iraqi war.

569
00:32:27.987 --> 00:32:32.617
I trust Congressman Crowley's judgment
because he has served this community.

570
00:32:32.700 --> 00:32:34.369
I built a great partnership

571
00:32:34.452 --> 00:32:38.665
in making sure that whatever I needed
to do for this community,

572
00:32:38.748 --> 00:32:41.376
that the partner that I had
was the Congressman

573
00:32:41.459 --> 00:32:44.337
who has never failed to deliver
for this community.

574
00:32:45.630 --> 00:32:46.756
Thirty seconds.

575
00:32:46.839 --> 00:32:49.217
The vote for the Iraq war was disastrous.

576
00:32:49.676 --> 00:32:51.970
It destabilized an entire region.

577
00:32:52.053 --> 00:32:53.721
[crowd cheering and applauding]

578
00:32:54.973 --> 00:32:59.185
[moderator] What do you say about US role
towards Israel-Palestine,

579
00:32:59.394 --> 00:33:01.604
and moving the embassy and settlements?

580
00:33:03.231 --> 00:33:04.565
[exhales deeply] Yes.

581
00:33:04.983 --> 00:33:07.777
- No, this is a regular question.
- Oh, regular question. Oh!

582
00:33:07.860 --> 00:33:08.695
One minute.

583
00:33:08.778 --> 00:33:12.699
I believe that the Congressman
supports that position.

584
00:33:13.324 --> 00:33:16.285
That's it? Okay. Rebuttal.

585
00:33:16.369 --> 00:33:19.330
I think that moving the embassy--

586
00:33:19.414 --> 00:33:20.456
Uh...

587
00:33:20.540 --> 00:33:22.875
Moving the embassy
was a profound mistake

588
00:33:22.959 --> 00:33:24.544
in US foreign politics.

589
00:33:24.627 --> 00:33:25.628
[cheering and applauding]

590
00:33:25.712 --> 00:33:26.879
[Alexandria] Profound mistake!

591
00:33:28.047 --> 00:33:29.632
[Palma] Listen, you know, I--

592
00:33:29.716 --> 00:33:34.095
I would allow him to elaborate more
on his position on certain issues.

593
00:33:34.178 --> 00:33:35.680
[man] Tell him to come right here.

594
00:33:36.347 --> 00:33:38.683
And-- Again, right? We explained--

595
00:33:38.766 --> 00:33:43.062
We explained why he's not here,
and it's not disrespecting the Bronx.

596
00:33:43.646 --> 00:33:46.357
[Alexandria] So I wanna thank everyone
for participating in this process,

597
00:33:46.441 --> 00:33:50.570
because for so long,
we haven't had these forums.

598
00:33:50.653 --> 00:33:53.614
The only reason we are here
is because we organized

599
00:33:53.698 --> 00:33:56.951
for the first primary election in 14 years
in New York 14th.

600
00:33:57.035 --> 00:33:59.746
- For once, we have a choice.
- [scattered applause]

601
00:33:59.912 --> 00:34:01.456
For once, we have a choice.

602
00:34:01.539 --> 00:34:03.458
Okay. Thank you and good night.

603
00:34:03.958 --> 00:34:06.419
- Thank you.
- The candidates will stay--

604
00:34:06.502 --> 00:34:07.670
[man] Before you break.

605
00:34:08.337 --> 00:34:09.672
I have a question here.

606
00:34:09.756 --> 00:34:12.341
I think one of the questions
that was sent to you

607
00:34:12.425 --> 00:34:15.386
is of great concern
to the African community here.

608
00:34:15.470 --> 00:34:18.306
There's close to 300 businesses
in this district

609
00:34:18.389 --> 00:34:22.643
that are owned by Yemeni Americans
who are suffering from the travel ban--

610
00:34:22.727 --> 00:34:24.520
[moderator] Meantime, this last half hour,

611
00:34:24.604 --> 00:34:27.106
- you can go up to the candidates.
- But it said till eight o'clock.

612
00:34:29.400 --> 00:34:31.944
African immigrants,
they're feeling left out.

613
00:34:32.028 --> 00:34:35.406
Because most of them
have come here through the diversity visa.

614
00:34:35.490 --> 00:34:37.867
[man] Several of the individuals here
have families stuck

615
00:34:37.950 --> 00:34:40.036
outside the country for several months.

616
00:34:40.119 --> 00:34:43.039
I hope to have the honor of having
the support of the Yemeni community,

617
00:34:43.122 --> 00:34:45.416
but I also acknowledge
that I have to earn it and deserve it

618
00:34:45.500 --> 00:34:46.501
every single time.

619
00:34:46.584 --> 00:34:48.628
[Alexandria speaking Arabic]
Hello. Happy Eid.

620
00:34:48.711 --> 00:34:50.713
[in Arabic]
My name is Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez.

621
00:34:50.797 --> 00:34:51.631
I'm trying out here.

622
00:34:51.714 --> 00:34:54.759
I'm running for Congress out here
in New York's 14th Congressional district.

623
00:34:54.842 --> 00:34:59.222
And it is so important that we go out
and vote on Tuesday, June 26th.

624
00:35:07.605 --> 00:35:10.149
[moderator] If you agree with what--
you know, what I say,

625
00:35:10.233 --> 00:35:11.150
raise your hand.

626
00:35:11.234 --> 00:35:13.194
But if it's a no for you,
don't raise your hand.

627
00:35:13.277 --> 00:35:15.238
Support a woman's right to choose.

628
00:35:17.281 --> 00:35:20.660
Support the Paycheck Fairness Act
in eliminating the wage gap.

629
00:35:22.703 --> 00:35:23.663
Love this room.

630
00:35:23.746 --> 00:35:26.749
Support allowing transgender soldiers
to serve in the military.

631
00:35:28.209 --> 00:35:29.043
All right.

632
00:35:29.836 --> 00:35:33.131
Raise your hand if you refuse
to take corporate PAC money.

633
00:35:35.216 --> 00:35:37.135
- All right.
- [crowd cheering]

634
00:35:39.053 --> 00:35:41.305
[crowd] Amy, Amy!

635
00:35:41.389 --> 00:35:42.348
[moderator] All right.

636
00:35:46.519 --> 00:35:48.938
[Amy] Everybody that knows me
knows that on day one,

637
00:35:49.021 --> 00:35:53.484
I will co-sponsor HR-676 Medicare for all.

638
00:35:53.943 --> 00:35:58.239
It is time to join the other Democrats
in office right now

639
00:35:58.322 --> 00:36:00.408
that are standing up and saying,
"Enough is enough!

640
00:36:00.491 --> 00:36:02.660
We're not gonna accept
any more money from the hospital,

641
00:36:02.743 --> 00:36:04.328
the insurance companies and pharma,

642
00:36:04.745 --> 00:36:07.456
and we can have the people
behind the party again."

643
00:36:07.707 --> 00:36:10.001
- [crowd cheering and applauding]
- [moderator] Thank you.

644
00:36:12.545 --> 00:36:14.839
[Amy] They're actually now posting
that I'm a Marxist.

645
00:36:14.922 --> 00:36:16.382
I don't even know what a Marxist is.

646
00:36:17.049 --> 00:36:20.052
Commie, and socialist,
and a piece of shit,

647
00:36:20.136 --> 00:36:21.304
and a bitch.

648
00:36:21.512 --> 00:36:23.472
And, you know, I look like I'm Russian,

649
00:36:23.556 --> 00:36:25.808
go back to my country in Mexico...

650
00:36:26.309 --> 00:36:27.435
[chuckles]

651
00:36:28.561 --> 00:36:29.812
You know what? Bring it on.

652
00:36:33.733 --> 00:36:35.902
[woman]
Right now we could really use your help.

653
00:36:35.985 --> 00:36:39.155
We are a completely
grassroots-powered movement.

654
00:36:39.238 --> 00:36:41.324
It's gonna be a close race.

655
00:36:41.407 --> 00:36:43.701
It's, you know,
obviously it's a crowded field.

656
00:36:43.784 --> 00:36:46.412
Oh, hell, yeah!
Okay, that's what I like to hear.

657
00:36:50.124 --> 00:36:52.752
I'm cleaning up after a bunch of kids.
[chuckles]

658
00:36:53.127 --> 00:36:55.087
I'm the house mom, the den mother.

659
00:36:55.504 --> 00:36:56.839
So I'm gonna make this disclaimer.

660
00:36:56.923 --> 00:36:59.884
I'm Amy Vilela.
I do not approve of this mess.

661
00:37:00.218 --> 00:37:01.344
[all laughing]

662
00:37:08.309 --> 00:37:10.645
My name's Amy,
and I am not a career politician.

663
00:37:10.728 --> 00:37:11.562
Yes.

664
00:37:11.646 --> 00:37:14.023
I was someone that should not be able
to run for Congress.

665
00:37:14.106 --> 00:37:16.901
I was a single mom.
I was on Medicaid, WIC, food stamps.

666
00:37:16.984 --> 00:37:17.818
That's right.

667
00:37:17.902 --> 00:37:19.904
Worked my way through college somehow,

668
00:37:19.987 --> 00:37:22.365
- and became a Chief Financial Officer.
- Cool.

669
00:37:22.698 --> 00:37:24.825
- [chuckles] Thanks a lot.
- No problem, take care.

670
00:37:25.660 --> 00:37:26.869
[Amy] This is not a game to me.

671
00:37:27.495 --> 00:37:29.747
This is not an opportunistic move for me.

672
00:37:29.830 --> 00:37:30.748
You got a good grip.

673
00:37:31.249 --> 00:37:34.877
I turned my back
on an executive level job.

674
00:37:35.294 --> 00:37:36.837
I sold my house.

675
00:37:36.921 --> 00:37:38.965
I've gone into debt.

676
00:37:41.300 --> 00:37:43.135
In the beginning, it was a tough decision,

677
00:37:43.552 --> 00:37:45.429
but I would do it again
in a heartbeat now.

678
00:37:47.765 --> 00:37:50.226
[woman 1] I'm scared too,
because I've never felt this confident...

679
00:37:51.560 --> 00:37:52.687
in a campaign.

680
00:37:52.770 --> 00:37:55.398
[woman 2] It makes me uncomfortable,
but I feel like we might win.

681
00:37:55.481 --> 00:37:58.025
Every step of the way,
we seem to be on track.

682
00:37:58.109 --> 00:37:59.610
[Joz] We're like in the Amazing Race...

683
00:37:59.694 --> 00:38:02.697
And no one else believes in the path,
so they think we're not on track.

684
00:38:02.780 --> 00:38:04.573
They say, you know,
"You don't have a shot

685
00:38:04.657 --> 00:38:08.327
if you don't raise $100,000
between now and next week,

686
00:38:08.411 --> 00:38:10.746
or you don't have a shot
if you're not gonna put out

687
00:38:10.830 --> 00:38:12.331
targeted mailers, you know,

688
00:38:12.415 --> 00:38:15.918
to women between the ages
of 55 and 65 before this date.

689
00:38:16.210 --> 00:38:19.380
After 2016, nothing is predictable.
Nothing!

690
00:38:19.463 --> 00:38:20.589
[uplifting music playing]

691
00:38:24.218 --> 00:38:26.470
Hi, Amin, this is Amy Vilela.

692
00:38:26.554 --> 00:38:31.392
I was just actually doing
my fundraising calls and saw your name.

693
00:38:31.475 --> 00:38:34.061
Didn't know if you'd be able
to help us out one last time.

694
00:38:35.813 --> 00:38:38.399
- [Amin] I'm not sure if I can.
- Okay.

695
00:38:39.358 --> 00:38:40.359
[Amy sighs]

696
00:38:41.235 --> 00:38:42.695
Did I catch you at a bad time?

697
00:38:42.945 --> 00:38:45.948
[Amy] You know, even $50
would be absolutely amazing.

698
00:38:46.032 --> 00:38:49.702
[man] Really not in a position
to go much further, I'm afraid...

699
00:38:49.785 --> 00:38:50.661
[Amy] Sure.

700
00:38:51.620 --> 00:38:52.580
[sighs deeply]

701
00:38:52.663 --> 00:38:53.748
[Amy] Thank you so much.

702
00:38:53.831 --> 00:38:55.708
- What can I put you guys down for?
- [woman] Okay.

703
00:38:55.791 --> 00:38:58.461
We would donate $100.

704
00:38:58.544 --> 00:39:00.796
Thank you so, so much, guys.

705
00:39:01.005 --> 00:39:02.673
That really made my day.

706
00:39:13.934 --> 00:39:14.935
[spraying]

707
00:39:20.107 --> 00:39:21.442
[Shannon] So, I was getting curious

708
00:39:21.525 --> 00:39:23.903
where some of our opponents
are getting their money.

709
00:39:24.945 --> 00:39:25.863
Particularly Horsford.

710
00:39:25.946 --> 00:39:28.449
He's not taking money
from Big Pharma, but...

711
00:39:28.657 --> 00:39:29.575
[chuckles]

712
00:39:29.658 --> 00:39:33.120
I've managed to see
who he is taking money from.

713
00:39:33.204 --> 00:39:37.249
Culinary Union,
executives from MGM and Tropicana,

714
00:39:37.625 --> 00:39:41.170
and the Crystal Sugar Agriculture Fund.

715
00:39:41.253 --> 00:39:42.171
Caesars,

716
00:39:43.047 --> 00:39:45.966
the Congressional Black Caucus,
Congressional Progressive Caucus,

717
00:39:46.342 --> 00:39:48.344
- and Joe Crowley.
- [music stops]

718
00:39:48.803 --> 00:39:53.682
Why is Joe Crowley
one of the leaders pushing this?

719
00:39:53.766 --> 00:39:55.976
He's pouring all this money into a race

720
00:39:56.060 --> 00:39:59.605
that is two weeks
before the New York primary.

721
00:40:00.648 --> 00:40:04.693
[Shannon]
If Amy wins against the establishment Dem,

722
00:40:05.194 --> 00:40:08.739
things look a little bit more hopeful
for the scrappy fighter from the Bronx.

723
00:40:09.615 --> 00:40:10.741
[emotional music playing]

724
00:40:21.127 --> 00:40:22.837
[Amy] You know, I grew up in poverty.

725
00:40:23.045 --> 00:40:25.005
I was raised by a single mother.

726
00:40:25.423 --> 00:40:27.925
And so I learnt how to fight early on.

727
00:40:29.969 --> 00:40:31.762
Now that my eyes are open,

728
00:40:31.846 --> 00:40:35.599
I cannot and I will not close them again.

729
00:40:36.308 --> 00:40:38.561
[Cori] The person I'm running against
is complacent,

730
00:40:38.644 --> 00:40:39.895
but I'm not.

731
00:40:39.979 --> 00:40:43.607
I myself
can deescalate a person with a gun.

732
00:40:44.692 --> 00:40:47.820
And I'm not a police officer,
so I wonder how come they can't do it!

733
00:40:49.947 --> 00:40:52.199
[Alexandria]
I'm running because of Cori Bush.

734
00:40:52.992 --> 00:40:55.536
I'm running
because of Paula Jean Swearengin.

735
00:40:55.619 --> 00:40:57.955
I'm running because everyday Americans

736
00:40:58.038 --> 00:41:01.167
deserve to be represented
by everyday Americans.

737
00:41:01.667 --> 00:41:05.546
[Paula] And it's time for ordinary people
to do extraordinary things.

738
00:41:05.629 --> 00:41:07.923
Let's raise some hell,
and take our lives back.

739
00:41:08.007 --> 00:41:09.091
Thank you.

740
00:41:09.175 --> 00:41:10.801
[crowd cheering and applauding]

741
00:41:10.885 --> 00:41:12.178
[upbeat music playing]

742
00:41:12.428 --> 00:41:13.304
[Alexandria] Here we go.

743
00:41:13.387 --> 00:41:15.181
[woman 1] Nice to meet you.
All right. [laughs]

744
00:41:15.306 --> 00:41:16.891
[woman 2] Oh, we're in the middle--

745
00:41:16.974 --> 00:41:18.767
[indistinct chatter]

746
00:41:18.851 --> 00:41:20.394
[Cori Bush]
You guys gotta get used to this.

747
00:41:22.730 --> 00:41:23.898
[all laughing]

748
00:41:27.610 --> 00:41:28.986
[Alexandria] This thing is growing.

749
00:41:29.445 --> 00:41:30.946
It's-- It's happening.

750
00:41:35.075 --> 00:41:39.788
[Amy] We are beginning our world tour
through northern Nevada.

751
00:41:40.623 --> 00:41:41.957
This is a flip district.

752
00:41:42.541 --> 00:41:44.460
We have to keep on the issues,

753
00:41:44.543 --> 00:41:46.754
forget the party divides,
forget the parties,

754
00:41:46.837 --> 00:41:48.547
and let's talk about
what's really happening.

755
00:41:49.131 --> 00:41:51.634
But there are some things
I will not negotiate on.

756
00:41:52.051 --> 00:41:54.136
When we're talking about human life...

757
00:41:54.220 --> 00:41:57.264
human life,
and the ability just to survive

758
00:41:57.348 --> 00:41:59.475
and take care of your family and yourself,

759
00:41:59.558 --> 00:42:00.601
I will not buckle.

760
00:42:02.978 --> 00:42:03.812
Ouch!

761
00:42:05.940 --> 00:42:07.316
[Paula] We're clearing this area out.

762
00:42:07.399 --> 00:42:09.443
Ain't nobody been out here
for a couple years.

763
00:42:09.527 --> 00:42:11.195
So we can have this event out here.

764
00:42:14.448 --> 00:42:15.574
I look crazy!

765
00:42:17.952 --> 00:42:20.287
[Paula] I am a proud Appalachian woman.

766
00:42:20.371 --> 00:42:23.499
Because there's 10,000 miners
that marched that mountain,

767
00:42:23.958 --> 00:42:26.460
and stood up for us, our ancestors did.

768
00:42:26.544 --> 00:42:28.754
One of the biggest labor uprisings

769
00:42:29.171 --> 00:42:30.881
in our nation's history.

770
00:42:31.257 --> 00:42:33.259
This last year, I have hope.

771
00:42:33.342 --> 00:42:35.219
I have hope,
and it's because of all of you.

772
00:42:37.471 --> 00:42:40.558
[Cori] And I think about when you get
that new person on your job.

773
00:42:40.641 --> 00:42:42.726
I mean, you interview 'em,
and they're like so excited,

774
00:42:42.810 --> 00:42:44.812
like, "Oh, I'm gonna do this,
and I'm gonna do that,"

775
00:42:44.895 --> 00:42:46.897
and you think about the person
that was there before.

776
00:42:46.981 --> 00:42:48.816
How you couldn't get 'em
to move out their desk,

777
00:42:48.899 --> 00:42:51.151
- and they were always calling off, and--
- [all laughing]

778
00:42:51.235 --> 00:42:54.530
If we can get even just 15 more people
across the country

779
00:42:54.613 --> 00:42:58.826
that will come in in 2019 and perform,

780
00:42:59.034 --> 00:43:01.954
how much greater will we be?
And then in two more years, 30.

781
00:43:02.037 --> 00:43:05.374
You know, that's what I'm looking at.
Like, it's just continuing to grow.

782
00:43:05.749 --> 00:43:08.377
- [crowd cheering]
- I'm running to represent the Bronx.

783
00:43:08.460 --> 00:43:10.087
I'm a third generation Bronxite.

784
00:43:10.170 --> 00:43:11.964
I'm a Latina, I'm a Boricua,

785
00:43:12.047 --> 00:43:14.133
I'm a descendant of Taino Indians.

786
00:43:14.216 --> 00:43:16.343
I am a descendant of African slaves.

787
00:43:16.427 --> 00:43:18.554
I am proud to be an American!

788
00:43:19.388 --> 00:43:21.765
But we have to rise to that promise.

789
00:43:27.271 --> 00:43:29.106
[woman]
That's your rice and that's my rice.

790
00:43:29.982 --> 00:43:31.275
Take a little tiny bite.

791
00:43:31.775 --> 00:43:32.735
[chuckles]

792
00:43:35.529 --> 00:43:36.614
[woman] The rice is good.

793
00:43:37.656 --> 00:43:40.242
Can you pass her the rice?

794
00:43:40.826 --> 00:43:44.330
[woman] When these two little monsters
were little.

795
00:43:44.413 --> 00:43:47.291
I don't know, she was maybe,
like, six or seven.

796
00:43:47.374 --> 00:43:48.709
She got upset and said,

797
00:43:49.835 --> 00:43:52.546
"You know what? I am leaving."

798
00:43:52.713 --> 00:43:53.881
[all laughing]

799
00:43:54.298 --> 00:43:55.924
- I'm like, "What?"
- "I've had enough."

800
00:43:56.008 --> 00:43:58.427
Yeah. I'm, like, "You what?"

801
00:43:58.510 --> 00:43:59.970
"Yeah, I'm gonna leave."

802
00:44:00.054 --> 00:44:01.722
I say, "Okay."

803
00:44:02.348 --> 00:44:04.058
She went, got the suitcase,

804
00:44:04.141 --> 00:44:06.018
put all her clothes there.

805
00:44:06.352 --> 00:44:09.104
She found a nice hat, she put it on.

806
00:44:09.563 --> 00:44:11.815
She went to the curb,

807
00:44:11.899 --> 00:44:13.734
and she's looking both sides,

808
00:44:14.652 --> 00:44:17.196
like, "Hmm... Which way I go?"

809
00:44:17.279 --> 00:44:18.530
[all laughing]

810
00:44:18.697 --> 00:44:22.034
So one thing Sandy always had,
it was free will.

811
00:44:27.247 --> 00:44:30.751
[male voice]
So, here we have Alexandria Ocasio.

812
00:44:31.085 --> 00:44:33.087
[applauding]

813
00:44:33.420 --> 00:44:35.422
A SPECIAL DAY

814
00:44:44.848 --> 00:44:46.058
[Alexandria] For our next thing,

815
00:44:46.850 --> 00:44:49.937
we will take a little look at aliens.

816
00:44:50.229 --> 00:44:53.357
Aliens, this is our aliens issue.

817
00:44:54.024 --> 00:44:55.901
[screaming]

818
00:44:56.026 --> 00:44:57.778
It's taking me down!

819
00:44:58.153 --> 00:44:59.405
[Alexandria] When I was a kid,

820
00:44:59.988 --> 00:45:04.702
about half of New York City high-schoolers
would never graduate.

821
00:45:07.037 --> 00:45:10.165
And so, when I was about four,
five years old,

822
00:45:10.958 --> 00:45:13.752
my whole extended family
chipped in everything they could

823
00:45:13.836 --> 00:45:17.506
for a small down payment on a tiny house
about 30 minutes north,

824
00:45:17.589 --> 00:45:19.717
in a place called Yorktown.

825
00:45:37.860 --> 00:45:40.154
I went to public school

826
00:45:40.237 --> 00:45:43.574
in a town where no one looked like me.

827
00:45:46.034 --> 00:45:47.286
And then,

828
00:45:47.369 --> 00:45:50.414
always, always driving down to the Bronx,

829
00:45:51.457 --> 00:45:55.043
because all of my family
and our family friends

830
00:45:55.127 --> 00:45:56.420
were still in the Bronx.

831
00:46:01.300 --> 00:46:04.219
My Mom cleaned houses growing up.

832
00:46:06.930 --> 00:46:12.478
She would clean a woman's home
in exchange for SAT lessons for me.

833
00:46:17.107 --> 00:46:19.777
My father knew my soul

834
00:46:19.860 --> 00:46:22.029
better than anyone on this planet.

835
00:46:24.990 --> 00:46:30.204
He really made me believe
that I had true power in this world.

836
00:46:39.421 --> 00:46:42.132
My Dad passed away when I was in college.

837
00:46:46.637 --> 00:46:49.515
Losing him in a time

838
00:46:49.598 --> 00:46:51.934
when you're just figuring out the world

839
00:46:52.017 --> 00:46:53.936
was really hard.

840
00:46:56.396 --> 00:46:57.940
I didn't know up from down.

841
00:47:03.403 --> 00:47:05.906
[woman 1] If you were to find out,
okay, this is coming.

842
00:47:05.989 --> 00:47:08.575
We were digging back,
and then there we were.

843
00:47:08.659 --> 00:47:10.494
We make a lot of holes, and then--

844
00:47:10.577 --> 00:47:12.871
[woman 2] Wait, so he left home,
but what did you do?

845
00:47:12.955 --> 00:47:14.581
[man] Two week extensions.

846
00:47:14.915 --> 00:47:16.333
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

847
00:47:19.545 --> 00:47:20.671
[music fades out]

848
00:47:23.340 --> 00:47:25.092
- [Alexandria] Thank you.
- [Riley] Let it roll.

849
00:47:25.175 --> 00:47:26.260
Thank you.

850
00:47:30.514 --> 00:47:31.890
[Alexandria] So look at this thing.

851
00:47:32.140 --> 00:47:36.103
Everybody in the district
got this Victoria's Secret catalogue

852
00:47:36.687 --> 00:47:38.313
of my opponent.

853
00:47:39.189 --> 00:47:42.526
I mean, I'm not trying to gas myself up,
or brag, or anything,

854
00:47:42.609 --> 00:47:46.029
but this is the difference
between an organizer and a strategist.

855
00:47:46.697 --> 00:47:48.448
What am I trying to get people to do?

856
00:47:49.116 --> 00:47:51.702
Two things: I want them to know my name,

857
00:47:51.910 --> 00:47:53.620
and I want them to know
that they need to vote.

858
00:47:53.704 --> 00:47:56.164
"Okay, vote for her, why?"

859
00:47:56.248 --> 00:47:59.209
"End the war on drugs.
A hundred percent renewable energy.

860
00:47:59.293 --> 00:48:00.836
Tuition-free public college."

861
00:48:00.919 --> 00:48:02.504
This is how a strategist does it.

862
00:48:02.963 --> 00:48:04.464
Where's the primary date on this?

863
00:48:05.299 --> 00:48:08.010
When you first see it, when you
first pull this out of your mailbox.

864
00:48:09.094 --> 00:48:12.681
"Taking on Donald Trump in Washington."

865
00:48:13.307 --> 00:48:16.143
"Delivering for Queens and the Bronx."

866
00:48:16.226 --> 00:48:19.146
Deliver is insider talk.

867
00:48:20.063 --> 00:48:23.525
Deliver means "pork."

868
00:48:24.192 --> 00:48:26.069
"Oh, Alex, you're being too harsh.

869
00:48:26.153 --> 00:48:27.946
You're being way too harsh.
Give him a chance."

870
00:48:28.030 --> 00:48:30.115
Okay, let's give him a chance.
Let's open it up.

871
00:48:31.158 --> 00:48:34.077
Okay, we got
this big, beautiful spread here.

872
00:48:34.161 --> 00:48:35.495
Where's the primary date?

873
00:48:37.080 --> 00:48:38.749
"Democrat Joe Crowley

874
00:48:38.832 --> 00:48:42.085
is leading the fight
against Donald Trump."

875
00:48:45.255 --> 00:48:47.841
There is nothing
about the path forward here.

876
00:48:48.550 --> 00:48:50.302
Trump: three times,

877
00:48:50.636 --> 00:48:52.971
commitments: zero times.

878
00:48:53.513 --> 00:48:56.141
One of these core, core issues

879
00:48:56.224 --> 00:48:57.935
for the Democratic establishment

880
00:48:58.018 --> 00:49:00.979
is that their consultants are garbage.

881
00:49:01.063 --> 00:49:02.439
- [Alexandria sighs]
- They're losing.

882
00:49:02.522 --> 00:49:06.902
It's scary that this is, like, the fourth
most powerful Democrat in the country,

883
00:49:06.985 --> 00:49:09.363
and this is the type of stuff
that he's doing?

884
00:49:13.867 --> 00:49:15.953
[Alexandria] I like--
I like this one.

885
00:49:17.663 --> 00:49:19.748
- This looks like you're on a frontline.
- [Saikat] Right.

886
00:49:19.831 --> 00:49:21.291
- And that's where we are.
- [Saikat] I like it.

887
00:49:21.375 --> 00:49:22.876
[Alexandria] So what I'd like--

888
00:49:22.960 --> 00:49:25.253
What I kind of envisioned
for the back of these things is...

889
00:49:25.420 --> 00:49:28.006
three words or four words

890
00:49:28.674 --> 00:49:30.509
- that are big on the back.
- Okay.

891
00:49:30.592 --> 00:49:33.845
So whether it's, like,
"Medicare for all"...

892
00:49:33.929 --> 00:49:34.763
Okay.

893
00:49:34.846 --> 00:49:37.933
[Saikat] I think "rejecting the corporate
money" is like a big-- it's like a signal,

894
00:49:38.016 --> 00:49:39.768
sort of a big signal, you know.

895
00:49:39.851 --> 00:49:40.852
[Alexandria] Yeah, it is.

896
00:49:40.936 --> 00:49:43.438
And I think it's stronger
than the word "progressive".

897
00:49:43.522 --> 00:49:46.566
"Abolish ICE" is another one
that we could go to, if you wanted.

898
00:49:46.650 --> 00:49:48.068
I mean, it's kinda cool.

899
00:49:48.151 --> 00:49:51.989
Like, I wouldn't mind having posters
that say "abolish ICE".

900
00:49:52.072 --> 00:49:52.906
Yeah.

901
00:49:52.990 --> 00:49:55.492
To be in a political poster.
They're gonna say, "That's gangster!"

902
00:49:55.575 --> 00:49:57.327
- Like, up in Jackson Heights.
- Yeah.

903
00:49:57.953 --> 00:49:59.079
[whimsical music playing]

904
00:50:02.541 --> 00:50:04.418
I'll show you what your ads look like.

905
00:50:04.835 --> 00:50:07.713
We got women, unions,
criminal justice, under 25,

906
00:50:07.796 --> 00:50:10.632
- over 55, Obama...
- Great.

907
00:50:11.842 --> 00:50:13.719
Bengali, English, Spanish.

908
00:50:16.513 --> 00:50:18.056
- Sounds good.
- It's moving.

909
00:50:18.473 --> 00:50:20.142
So if you're still
following along with us,

910
00:50:20.225 --> 00:50:22.811
I'm gonna plug our Bronx rally again.

911
00:50:22.894 --> 00:50:25.731
It's this Sunday, 2 p.m.,
Allerton Avenue.

912
00:50:26.231 --> 00:50:27.733
Come out, bring your friends...

913
00:50:46.793 --> 00:50:49.421
[Alexandria] All right, guys, today is

914
00:50:49.504 --> 00:50:54.051
the day of our first ever rally.

915
00:50:54.134 --> 00:50:55.385
[crowd cheering]

916
00:50:56.219 --> 00:50:58.263
[Alexandria]
Who's willing to lead an event,

917
00:50:58.597 --> 00:51:00.057
and have a couple friends over

918
00:51:00.140 --> 00:51:02.392
to do any one of the things
that we just talked about?

919
00:51:02.476 --> 00:51:04.436
Raise your hand
if you're willing to do it.

920
00:51:04.519 --> 00:51:06.813
All right. All right.

921
00:51:07.814 --> 00:51:10.358
Now, if you just raised your hand,
I want you to stand up.

922
00:51:11.068 --> 00:51:12.027
Got 'em.

923
00:51:13.987 --> 00:51:15.989
Applause. Give them a round of applause.

924
00:51:18.533 --> 00:51:21.578
[Alexandria] Everybody in this room
knows five to ten people.

925
00:51:22.037 --> 00:51:25.082
And then those five to ten people
know another five to ten people.

926
00:51:25.457 --> 00:51:28.460
Frankly, big money is very lonely,

927
00:51:28.543 --> 00:51:31.088
and we've got people on our side.

928
00:51:31.171 --> 00:51:33.173
[crowd cheering and applauding]

929
00:51:34.674 --> 00:51:38.678
[in Spanish]
The Machine here imposes candidates.

930
00:51:38.762 --> 00:51:43.266
But all they want are puppets
to do their bidding.

931
00:51:43.350 --> 00:51:45.393
And we're tired of that.

932
00:51:45.477 --> 00:51:49.481
[in Spanish] Because everybody in this
community deserves...

933
00:51:49.564 --> 00:51:51.566
[in English] ...health care. [chuckles]

934
00:51:52.192 --> 00:51:55.862
[in Spanish] Up with Alexandria!
Down with the Machine!

935
00:51:56.154 --> 00:51:58.949
[woman] I'm here
with the Democratic Socialists of America,

936
00:51:59.032 --> 00:52:03.036
and we're so proud
to endorse Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez today.

937
00:52:03.328 --> 00:52:06.081
[Alexandria] The entire New York
political establishment,

938
00:52:06.665 --> 00:52:08.750
they have no idea what's going on.

939
00:52:09.459 --> 00:52:12.129
This race is winnable.

940
00:52:12.671 --> 00:52:14.756
And don't let anybody tell you
any different,

941
00:52:15.215 --> 00:52:19.052
because the power out there,
I'm telling you, is an illusion.

942
00:52:19.928 --> 00:52:23.723
[man] This election is extremely close.
Every vote counts.

943
00:52:23.807 --> 00:52:25.809
So please, please get out to vote.

944
00:52:25.892 --> 00:52:27.644
Can we count on your support for Alex?

945
00:52:28.895 --> 00:52:29.896
Awesome.

946
00:52:31.022 --> 00:52:32.899
[woman]
Alexandria is a progressive Democrat

947
00:52:32.983 --> 00:52:34.276
who's not taking any corporate donations.

948
00:52:34.359 --> 00:52:36.820
[woman]
We've got people, and they've got money.

949
00:52:37.404 --> 00:52:40.866
[man] ...Chester Road, and if you go
all the way down Pelham Parkway,

950
00:52:41.616 --> 00:52:42.909
these are all businesses.

951
00:52:43.160 --> 00:52:45.996
- There you go.
- Yes!

952
00:52:46.955 --> 00:52:48.248
Thank you.

953
00:52:50.834 --> 00:52:55.547
[Alexandria]
Things are accelerating so quickly.

954
00:52:55.964 --> 00:53:00.051
We released our campaign video on Tuesday,

955
00:53:00.135 --> 00:53:01.344
it just...

956
00:53:02.137 --> 00:53:02.971
blew up.

957
00:53:08.643 --> 00:53:12.105
[Alexandria]
Before we knew it, we passed $200,000.

958
00:53:12.189 --> 00:53:13.815
And, like, that's a race.

959
00:53:15.734 --> 00:53:17.527
But the question is...

960
00:53:19.738 --> 00:53:22.616
...has this all come
with enough time to make a difference?

961
00:53:33.793 --> 00:53:34.794
[music stops]

962
00:53:37.339 --> 00:53:40.759
[Alexandria]
He's operating in his kingdom, you know.

963
00:53:41.134 --> 00:53:43.553
They don't let us into senior centers,

964
00:53:43.637 --> 00:53:45.639
but they roll open the red carpet for him.

965
00:53:45.722 --> 00:53:46.973
I mean, look at this.

966
00:53:47.724 --> 00:53:51.228
He has, like, every endorsement ever.

967
00:53:53.063 --> 00:53:54.022
So we'll see.

968
00:53:55.774 --> 00:53:58.276
New York! New York!

969
00:53:58.526 --> 00:54:00.820
Show us your pride!

970
00:54:10.664 --> 00:54:14.167
[crowd] Who you gonna vote for?
Ocasio-Cortez!

971
00:54:14.376 --> 00:54:17.879
Who you gonna vote for?
Ocasio-Cortez!

972
00:54:17.963 --> 00:54:19.631
[in Spanish] The primary, this June 26th.

973
00:54:19.714 --> 00:54:22.467
The first primary election in 14 years.

974
00:54:22.842 --> 00:54:24.427
Healthcare is a right!

975
00:54:24.511 --> 00:54:27.305
Fight, fight, fight!
Healthcare is a right!

976
00:54:27.389 --> 00:54:28.932
Yes, healthcare is a right.

977
00:54:29.432 --> 00:54:30.934
And we gotta get some.

978
00:54:31.559 --> 00:54:33.311
- [upbeat music playing]
- [indistinct chatter]

979
00:54:41.444 --> 00:54:43.029
Haven't seen you in a while.

980
00:54:43.822 --> 00:54:46.992
- Re-election time, huh?
- Hey, you know, every so often.

981
00:54:47.075 --> 00:54:48.702
You're going to do it. Don't worry.

982
00:54:48.785 --> 00:54:50.829
Hey, Bill. How are you, brother?

983
00:54:50.912 --> 00:54:51.913
You are in my district.

984
00:54:51.997 --> 00:54:54.958
[man] I don't know who that
stupid woman is, but I'm voting for you.

985
00:54:55.041 --> 00:54:56.084
Thank you.

986
00:54:57.210 --> 00:54:59.004
She's not stupid, but thank you.

987
00:54:59.087 --> 00:55:01.464
She may not be stupid,
but I don't like her anyway.

988
00:55:01.548 --> 00:55:02.424
Good luck, Joe.

989
00:55:03.174 --> 00:55:05.093
[man]
Adrienne, can you send me a copy of it?

990
00:55:05.510 --> 00:55:07.345
- Yeah, we'll send you a copy.
- Come on in, Bill.

991
00:55:09.180 --> 00:55:12.392
[Alexandria]
Yesterday, I get this phone call.

992
00:55:13.977 --> 00:55:17.564
The guy who won't show up
to a hundred-person community debate

993
00:55:17.647 --> 00:55:21.735
has now agreed to debate me
live on New York One.

994
00:55:21.818 --> 00:55:23.153
Hi, how you doin'?

995
00:55:26.197 --> 00:55:28.908
[Alexandria]
I am, frankly, a little scared.

996
00:55:31.453 --> 00:55:34.998
This is either a really big misstep
that they've made,

997
00:55:35.332 --> 00:55:37.792
or they have this really well planned.

998
00:56:02.776 --> 00:56:05.695
[Amy] When you're running for office,
and you're not, you know,

999
00:56:05.779 --> 00:56:07.030
a typical candidate...

1000
00:56:08.698 --> 00:56:10.492
you have to remember your "why."

1001
00:56:10.950 --> 00:56:12.327
Why are you doing it?

1002
00:56:16.289 --> 00:56:18.833
[Amy] I remember actually
the day I put this stone here.

1003
00:56:19.417 --> 00:56:21.169
It would have been two years ago.

1004
00:56:22.087 --> 00:56:24.047
And it seems like decades ago.

1005
00:56:25.256 --> 00:56:26.883
And at that point...

1006
00:56:27.842 --> 00:56:29.135
I was so lost.

1007
00:56:31.554 --> 00:56:34.224
She didn't wanna be left in one place.

1008
00:56:34.599 --> 00:56:37.060
So, as crazy as it makes me look,

1009
00:56:37.143 --> 00:56:38.478
I follow her wishes.

1010
00:56:38.561 --> 00:56:40.605
You wanna travel? We're gonna travel.

1011
00:56:42.690 --> 00:56:45.735
I don't want this
to go flying around in the car. [laughs]

1012
00:56:46.569 --> 00:56:47.654
That wouldn't be cool.

1013
00:56:52.242 --> 00:56:53.410
[somber music playing]

1014
00:57:30.447 --> 00:57:32.740
[Amy] When the time came,
they said she was brain-dead.

1015
00:57:35.076 --> 00:57:37.579
I knew something was wrong
'cause my sister Ellie had gone over--

1016
00:57:37.662 --> 00:57:38.621
She's an RN.

1017
00:57:39.581 --> 00:57:42.083
And I looked to my sister
and said, "No, Ellie.

1018
00:57:42.876 --> 00:57:45.044
No. Don't-- No."

1019
00:57:45.503 --> 00:57:48.465
And I saw for the first time,
my sister, she just--

1020
00:57:50.592 --> 00:57:52.093
I knew she was gone.

1021
00:58:00.018 --> 00:58:02.020
But she still was alive for a while.

1022
00:58:02.103 --> 00:58:03.938
So, I got into bed and I held her,

1023
00:58:04.022 --> 00:58:07.275
and I sang this song I used to sing to her
when she was a baby. [sobs]

1024
00:58:10.737 --> 00:58:12.864
I played with her hair and told her
how much I loved her

1025
00:58:12.947 --> 00:58:15.033
until she took her last breath.

1026
00:58:25.919 --> 00:58:27.629
[Amy] It's not just our family.

1027
00:58:27.837 --> 00:58:31.090
It's 30,000 families a year.

1028
00:58:32.675 --> 00:58:36.513
Thirty thousand of us a year...

1029
00:58:37.680 --> 00:58:41.476
that are losing loved ones
because they don't have insurance.

1030
00:58:47.065 --> 00:58:49.400
No one in this great country

1031
00:58:50.276 --> 00:58:53.112
should be dying
because they don't understand

1032
00:58:53.196 --> 00:58:55.448
the intricate system of insurance.

1033
00:58:55.782 --> 00:58:57.325
And why is it difficult?

1034
00:58:57.742 --> 00:59:00.745
Because of algorithms,
because of risk assessment,

1035
00:59:01.412 --> 00:59:02.956
because the CFOs

1036
00:59:04.249 --> 00:59:06.459
that work in that field

1037
00:59:07.460 --> 00:59:11.631
are sitting there figuring out ways
to make optimum profit

1038
00:59:12.173 --> 00:59:13.633
for their shareholders.

1039
00:59:15.802 --> 00:59:18.471
This is a commodity.

1040
00:59:21.057 --> 00:59:22.767
My daughter's life wasn't.

1041
00:59:30.233 --> 00:59:33.111
We all handle it differently.
A lot of grieving parents

1042
00:59:33.194 --> 00:59:35.905
that I've been in contact with,
they're sort of, like,

1043
00:59:35.989 --> 00:59:39.450
really shocked that I'm able to do
as much as I'm doing.

1044
00:59:43.997 --> 00:59:45.540
For me, it's "How can I not?"

1045
00:59:47.208 --> 00:59:48.585
I will never stop.

1046
00:59:49.294 --> 00:59:53.006
I'm not going to allow my daughter

1047
00:59:54.007 --> 00:59:56.634
to have died for nothing.

1048
00:59:57.051 --> 00:59:58.177
[somber music playing]

1049
01:00:16.029 --> 01:00:19.699
[Alexandria] I started waitressing
when the financial crisis hit.

1050
01:00:24.537 --> 01:00:26.456
My Dad had passed away.

1051
01:00:27.915 --> 01:00:32.837
Working in a non-profit
just wasn't enough for our family,

1052
01:00:32.920 --> 01:00:34.631
because we were about to lose our home.

1053
01:00:37.258 --> 01:00:40.928
You're trying to make
a $300 student loan bill here,

1054
01:00:41.012 --> 01:00:43.514
and your foreclosure installment here...

1055
01:00:44.515 --> 01:00:46.893
You just do your best to survive.

1056
01:00:53.650 --> 01:00:57.153
That's been the reality
for millions of people in this country.

1057
01:00:57.570 --> 01:01:00.031
That feel like they're just hanging
by a thread.

1058
01:01:01.324 --> 01:01:03.785
And they feel
like no one's fighting for them,

1059
01:01:03.868 --> 01:01:06.245
and everyone's just in it for themselves.

1060
01:01:20.176 --> 01:01:23.012
I am debating
on behalf of the movement tonight.

1061
01:01:27.684 --> 01:01:30.645
This is not about electing me to Congress,

1062
01:01:30.728 --> 01:01:33.272
this is about electing us to Congress.

1063
01:01:35.441 --> 01:01:36.984
That's what I have to communicate.

1064
01:01:39.362 --> 01:01:41.155
I need to take up space.

1065
01:01:41.364 --> 01:01:42.865
I need to take up space.

1066
01:01:43.741 --> 01:01:44.784
I am here.

1067
01:01:48.287 --> 01:01:50.164
- [Alexandria chuckles]
- [Riley] What's that?

1068
01:01:50.248 --> 01:01:52.291
I don't know. This is me takin' up space.

1069
01:01:53.251 --> 01:01:54.377
Sending your energies out.

1070
01:01:54.460 --> 01:01:56.337
- I'm gonna try it tonight--
- Aura building.

1071
01:01:59.090 --> 01:02:00.091
[sighs]

1072
01:02:10.393 --> 01:02:12.395
- I can do this.
- I know you can.

1073
01:02:12.854 --> 01:02:15.565
I am experienced enough to do this.

1074
01:02:17.275 --> 01:02:19.777
I am knowledgeable enough to do this.

1075
01:02:20.528 --> 01:02:23.114
I am prepared enough to do this.

1076
01:02:24.198 --> 01:02:26.909
I am mature enough to do this.

1077
01:02:27.910 --> 01:02:29.787
I am brave enough to do this.

1078
01:02:32.081 --> 01:02:34.000
And this whole thing,

1079
01:02:34.083 --> 01:02:36.669
this whole time
he's gonna tell me I can't do this.

1080
01:02:36.753 --> 01:02:38.337
He's gonna tell me I'm small,

1081
01:02:38.421 --> 01:02:40.089
that I'm little, that I'm young,

1082
01:02:40.173 --> 01:02:41.674
that I'm inexperienced...

1083
01:02:44.552 --> 01:02:45.428
[exhales loudly]

1084
01:02:46.721 --> 01:02:48.055
["Inside City Hall" intro]

1085
01:02:50.475 --> 01:02:51.976
[man] Welcome back to Inside City Hall

1086
01:02:52.059 --> 01:02:54.020
where we are holding
a Democratic Primary debate

1087
01:02:54.103 --> 01:02:56.189
for the 14th Congressional District.

1088
01:02:56.272 --> 01:02:59.734
Joe Crowley has represented this area
in Congress since 1999.

1089
01:02:59.817 --> 01:03:02.528
He's being challenged
by Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez.

1090
01:03:02.612 --> 01:03:03.863
She is a native of the Bronx

1091
01:03:03.946 --> 01:03:06.949
who has worked as political aid
and as an activist.

1092
01:03:07.325 --> 01:03:08.910
Here are tonight's rules. Each can--

1093
01:03:08.993 --> 01:03:11.996
For over 20 years,
the interests of working families

1094
01:03:12.079 --> 01:03:15.249
have been sold off
to luxury real estate developers,

1095
01:03:15.333 --> 01:03:16.459
Wall Street banks,

1096
01:03:16.542 --> 01:03:19.003
and for-profit healthcare corporations.

1097
01:03:19.086 --> 01:03:20.379
And for 20 years,

1098
01:03:20.463 --> 01:03:22.089
our rents have been going up,

1099
01:03:22.173 --> 01:03:24.467
healthcare has been getting
more expensive,

1100
01:03:24.550 --> 01:03:26.636
and our incomes are staying the same.

1101
01:03:27.011 --> 01:03:28.971
Not all Democrats are the same.

1102
01:03:29.055 --> 01:03:31.766
And in a district
that is overwhelmingly working class,

1103
01:03:31.849 --> 01:03:33.935
we deserve a working class champion.

1104
01:03:34.018 --> 01:03:35.812
Okay. Thank you. Mr. Crowley?

1105
01:03:35.895 --> 01:03:38.147
First of all,
thanks a lot for this evening.

1106
01:03:38.231 --> 01:03:41.067
It's great to see you, and thanks
for participating this evening as well.

1107
01:03:41.150 --> 01:03:43.110
I think it's a good opportunity for us
to have an--

1108
01:03:43.194 --> 01:03:44.779
an exchange of thoughts and ideas

1109
01:03:44.862 --> 01:03:46.989
about the issues of concern
to our constituency.

1110
01:03:47.073 --> 01:03:49.867
If a person loves their community,
they would--

1111
01:03:49.951 --> 01:03:51.911
they would choose
to raise their family here.

1112
01:03:51.994 --> 01:03:54.038
They would choose
to send their kids to our schools.

1113
01:03:54.121 --> 01:03:56.624
When it comes to my kids,
I like to keep my kids out of it.

1114
01:03:56.707 --> 01:03:59.168
Quite frankly,
it's difficult to raise a family as it is.

1115
01:03:59.252 --> 01:04:01.212
This job requires me
to be away from them

1116
01:04:01.295 --> 01:04:04.006
for a very long time,
and I'm just doing the best that I can,

1117
01:04:04.090 --> 01:04:05.299
quite frankly, as a father.

1118
01:04:07.009 --> 01:04:10.179
I'm poised at this point and time
to take on this president.

1119
01:04:10.263 --> 01:04:12.306
And I've been doing it,
just like I did this week,

1120
01:04:12.390 --> 01:04:13.641
when I marched in protest

1121
01:04:13.724 --> 01:04:16.102
against what's happening
at our nation's border,

1122
01:04:16.519 --> 01:04:20.481
where this government
is separating children from their mothers.

1123
01:04:20.815 --> 01:04:23.276
It is antithetical
to everything that I believe in,

1124
01:04:23.359 --> 01:04:25.570
- and that's what I fight for everyday.
- And you know what?

1125
01:04:25.653 --> 01:04:28.990
If this organization is as fascist
as you have called it...

1126
01:04:29.073 --> 01:04:31.242
- I've said it's fascist.
- And you have said it's fascist.

1127
01:04:31.325 --> 01:04:34.954
Then why don't you
adopt the stance to eliminate it?

1128
01:04:35.037 --> 01:04:37.206
This is a moral problem.

1129
01:04:41.627 --> 01:04:42.628
[sighs deeply]

1130
01:04:47.633 --> 01:04:50.928
[woman]
It is the first big Primary day of 2018.

1131
01:04:51.012 --> 01:04:52.805
[man] Big Primary day.

1132
01:04:52.889 --> 01:04:55.099
- [man] All eyes on West Virginia.
- [woman] West Virginia.

1133
01:04:55.182 --> 01:04:56.392
[woman] You are on the clock.

1134
01:04:56.475 --> 01:04:58.603
It's Primary day there
and three other states...

1135
01:04:59.312 --> 01:05:01.647
[man speaks into megaphone]
It's not about red state issues,

1136
01:05:01.731 --> 01:05:03.941
it's not about blue state issues,
it's about people issues.

1137
01:05:04.025 --> 01:05:06.193
If Joe Manchin's got you down,
come on out,

1138
01:05:06.277 --> 01:05:08.112
bring a little bit of hope
back to West Virginia,

1139
01:05:08.195 --> 01:05:10.698
- and vote for Paula Jean Swearengin today.
- [Paula chuckles]

1140
01:05:10.781 --> 01:05:12.992
Votefor Paula Jean Swearengin today.

1141
01:05:13.075 --> 01:05:16.329
It's Primary day, West Virginia, 2018.

1142
01:05:17.580 --> 01:05:19.332
[man]
I would just like to report to everyone

1143
01:05:19.415 --> 01:05:22.585
that currently we have 35,000 votes
from some wonderful,

1144
01:05:22.668 --> 01:05:24.462
hardworking West Virginians just like you.

1145
01:05:24.545 --> 01:05:26.964
- So can we have a round of applause?
- [cheering and applauding]

1146
01:05:28.549 --> 01:05:30.843
[man] I'd like to thank you
for staying out with us so late,

1147
01:05:30.927 --> 01:05:33.596
enjoying a wonderful election night,
guys. So thank you.

1148
01:05:34.972 --> 01:05:37.975
[Paula] And these counties, these
bigger counties have not reported yet.

1149
01:05:38.392 --> 01:05:41.979
We've already got 42,000 votes.
We might still be able to win this

1150
01:05:42.605 --> 01:05:44.148
still, look at the bigger counties.

1151
01:05:44.607 --> 01:05:46.692
Manchin is 50,000 ahead of ya.

1152
01:05:46.776 --> 01:05:47.944
[indistinct chatter]

1153
01:05:49.862 --> 01:05:53.783
[man] Joe Manchin has been declared
the winner with 69.5% of the vote,

1154
01:05:54.075 --> 01:05:57.036
to Paula Jean Swearengin's
30.5% of the vote.

1155
01:05:57.119 --> 01:05:59.997
- [man] Not bad. Not bad.
- With 7.4% of the precincts reporting...

1156
01:06:03.542 --> 01:06:06.963
[woman] So, my thinking is,
if Paula was to run as independent,

1157
01:06:07.046 --> 01:06:09.382
if she has 30% of the Democratic vote,

1158
01:06:09.840 --> 01:06:12.510
there's possibility that she can pull over
some Independent

1159
01:06:12.593 --> 01:06:14.178
and even Republican voters.

1160
01:06:14.261 --> 01:06:17.765
If she runs as an Independent,
and Morrissey ends up

1161
01:06:17.848 --> 01:06:21.477
as our senator, you're done,
your political career is over.

1162
01:06:21.560 --> 01:06:23.729
[woman] I'm not telling you not to run
as an Independent,

1163
01:06:23.813 --> 01:06:25.106
because you are a good candidate,

1164
01:06:25.189 --> 01:06:28.192
and I believe in my heart
that people are drawn to good candidates.

1165
01:06:28.776 --> 01:06:32.071
I'm just asking you where you think
you can do the most good.

1166
01:06:33.322 --> 01:06:34.949
[phone ringing]

1167
01:06:35.032 --> 01:06:36.867
- Paula Jean? It's Mara.
- Hey, Mara.

1168
01:06:36.951 --> 01:06:38.452
Great race, by the way.

1169
01:06:38.536 --> 01:06:40.079
- Thank you, ma'am.
- Very good race.

1170
01:06:40.538 --> 01:06:42.623
Do you have a moment to talk
to Senator Manchin?

1171
01:06:42.707 --> 01:06:43.874
I sure do.

1172
01:06:43.958 --> 01:06:45.292
Okay. Hold on one second.

1173
01:06:46.127 --> 01:06:48.379
- Paula Jean?
- Hi, Senator Manchin.

1174
01:06:48.671 --> 01:06:50.464
How are you? I just wanna thank you.

1175
01:06:50.548 --> 01:06:53.718
I mean, I congratulate you. Thank you.
I've said to everybody...

1176
01:06:54.051 --> 01:06:56.512
You're just a classy person,
and I appreciate it very much.

1177
01:06:57.096 --> 01:06:59.348
Thank you, Senator Manchin,
that means a lot.

1178
01:06:59.432 --> 01:07:00.433
Whatever we can do--

1179
01:07:00.516 --> 01:07:03.185
If you wanna come in,
we could sit down and work together,

1180
01:07:03.269 --> 01:07:05.104
and look at some of the issues
you really have,

1181
01:07:05.187 --> 01:07:08.816
and see if we can come together
and try to help each other?

1182
01:07:09.150 --> 01:07:10.359
I'm happy to do that.

1183
01:07:11.527 --> 01:07:13.487
I'd love to come and sit down,
Senator Manchin.

1184
01:07:13.571 --> 01:07:15.114
I've been wanting that for a long time.

1185
01:07:15.197 --> 01:07:18.951
Okay. Why don't you-- You call Mara.
Mara has your number.

1186
01:07:19.201 --> 01:07:20.327
I'll have Mara schedule it,

1187
01:07:20.411 --> 01:07:22.955
that way we can spend some time together.
I'd love to do that.

1188
01:07:23.706 --> 01:07:24.540
Okay. Yeah.

1189
01:07:24.623 --> 01:07:27.084
I would like to see some changes
in this state, Senator Manchin.

1190
01:07:27.168 --> 01:07:28.419
- I understand.
- People are dyin'.

1191
01:07:29.712 --> 01:07:30.713
[emotional music playing]

1192
01:07:33.549 --> 01:07:34.925
[Cori] This is who we are.

1193
01:07:35.009 --> 01:07:36.719
This is the light of St. Louis.

1194
01:07:36.802 --> 01:07:38.554
We are the light of St. Louis.

1195
01:07:38.637 --> 01:07:39.638
Thank you all.

1196
01:07:39.722 --> 01:07:40.848
[crowd cheering]

1197
01:07:42.349 --> 01:07:43.809
[woman] Let's take a look at the race

1198
01:07:43.893 --> 01:07:45.978
for the Missouri
first Congressional district.

1199
01:07:46.312 --> 01:07:49.774
Incumbent Lacy Clay, 61% of the vote

1200
01:07:49.857 --> 01:07:53.277
against his closest competitor, Cori Bush,

1201
01:07:53.360 --> 01:07:55.196
with 31% percent of the vote.

1202
01:07:55.279 --> 01:07:59.283
It was the old guard
versus the progressive new guard.

1203
01:07:59.366 --> 01:08:02.203
Many of you
called for change after Ferguson.

1204
01:08:02.286 --> 01:08:04.705
It does not look
like that is happening, though.

1205
01:08:10.669 --> 01:08:12.046
[Amy] Feel like I'm gonna vomit.

1206
01:08:15.007 --> 01:08:17.343
...watching that screen pretty carefully
behind me...

1207
01:08:20.888 --> 01:08:22.765
[Amy]
I don't think I've seen Keenan this quiet.

1208
01:08:24.058 --> 01:08:24.892
Keenan,

1209
01:08:25.351 --> 01:08:29.522
you are the most awesome
campaign manager I could ask for.

1210
01:08:30.773 --> 01:08:32.858
We did this without knowing shit.

1211
01:08:33.025 --> 01:08:33.859
[Keenan laughs]

1212
01:08:34.318 --> 01:08:35.194
[Keenan] That's true.

1213
01:08:36.112 --> 01:08:38.906
[Amy] And you stuck through everything,
and were there, and I love you.

1214
01:08:39.615 --> 01:08:40.866
[Keenan] I love you too, Amy.

1215
01:08:45.746 --> 01:08:49.083
- [woman 1] Okay, we have new results.
- [woman 2] Next time I see you...

1216
01:08:51.210 --> 01:08:52.128
Shit.

1217
01:09:07.017 --> 01:09:08.686
[Amy] Some of us have gotta get through--

1218
01:09:09.770 --> 01:09:11.689
It's not about any one of us,
individually.

1219
01:09:11.772 --> 01:09:14.108
It's about the--the whole movement.

1220
01:09:15.025 --> 01:09:19.947
[Alexandria] It's just the reality that
in order for one of us to make it through,

1221
01:09:20.030 --> 01:09:21.907
100 of us have to try.

1222
01:09:22.992 --> 01:09:24.201
- [ends call]
- [sighs]

1223
01:09:28.372 --> 01:09:30.374
[woman]
Shalynne would be really proud of you.

1224
01:09:31.250 --> 01:09:32.626
[Amy crying]

1225
01:09:47.892 --> 01:09:49.727
[Shannon] I'm not sorry.

1226
01:09:51.270 --> 01:09:52.396
I'm not sorry.

1227
01:09:53.063 --> 01:09:54.940
I'm not sorry that I'm here.

1228
01:09:55.566 --> 01:09:59.069
I'm not sorry that we ran
the campaign that we ran.

1229
01:09:59.653 --> 01:10:02.156
The same reason that all of us are here,

1230
01:10:02.489 --> 01:10:03.949
that hasn't gone away.

1231
01:10:04.158 --> 01:10:06.202
The Primary ended,

1232
01:10:06.619 --> 01:10:08.704
the injustice has not.

1233
01:10:10.164 --> 01:10:12.166
We have a lot of work left to do,

1234
01:10:12.958 --> 01:10:15.878
and I for one am not gonna stop doing it

1235
01:10:16.503 --> 01:10:18.714
until we fix some things.

1236
01:10:25.346 --> 01:10:26.180
[music stops]

1237
01:10:39.401 --> 01:10:40.319
[sighs]

1238
01:10:44.531 --> 01:10:48.494
[Alexandria]
Thinking about 9 p.m. on Tuesday

1239
01:10:48.827 --> 01:10:52.206
makes me want to lock myself in a closet.

1240
01:10:55.000 --> 01:10:58.796
It will never be the same after Tuesday.

1241
01:10:58.879 --> 01:11:01.840
No matter what the outcome, this moment,

1242
01:11:03.384 --> 01:11:04.760
I'm never gonna get back.

1243
01:11:09.431 --> 01:11:10.391
[crowd cheering]

1244
01:11:12.268 --> 01:11:13.310
[man] Go, Lexie!

1245
01:11:14.645 --> 01:11:16.981
[moderator] Everyone, try to keep
the outbursts to a minimum,

1246
01:11:17.064 --> 01:11:18.983
we wanna keep this moving on
as best as we can.

1247
01:11:19.608 --> 01:11:22.027
So the New York Times editorial board
yesterday came out

1248
01:11:22.111 --> 01:11:25.572
- with a scathing review of your campaign--
- [Crowley] Wasn't that bad.

1249
01:11:25.656 --> 01:11:27.032
- [crowd laughing]
- ...calling, uh--

1250
01:11:27.116 --> 01:11:28.867
They criticize you for, quote,

1251
01:11:28.951 --> 01:11:31.412
"your galling snub of a debate
in the Bronx."

1252
01:11:31.495 --> 01:11:32.371
[crowd] That's right.

1253
01:11:32.454 --> 01:11:34.415
[moderator]
Is that the reason you're here tonight?

1254
01:11:34.498 --> 01:11:36.208
[Crowley] I appreciate the editorial

1255
01:11:36.292 --> 01:11:38.961
and I appreciate also the hard work
that I've been doing

1256
01:11:39.044 --> 01:11:41.547
to win back the support
of my constituents here.

1257
01:11:42.172 --> 01:11:45.467
When it comes time to standing up
against the big banks and Wall Street,

1258
01:11:45.843 --> 01:11:48.345
I stood shoulder to shoulder
with Barnie Frank

1259
01:11:48.429 --> 01:11:49.888
in passing Dod Frank.

1260
01:11:49.972 --> 01:11:52.224
I was proud of my work
and the effort that I did.

1261
01:11:52.308 --> 01:11:53.684
[cheering and applauding]

1262
01:11:53.767 --> 01:11:56.687
[Alexandria] So let's talk about
Dodd-Frank, because after the--

1263
01:11:56.770 --> 01:11:59.023
one of the directors
of Morgan Stanley emailed

1264
01:11:59.106 --> 01:12:01.358
my opponent's campaign director
and told him,

1265
01:12:01.692 --> 01:12:04.194
"Before you pass Dodd-Frank,
you need to kill the Lynch Amendment,"

1266
01:12:04.278 --> 01:12:05.988
which protects working families.

1267
01:12:06.697 --> 01:12:09.158
He left the floor of Congress,
went to that fundraiser,

1268
01:12:09.241 --> 01:12:12.828
came back and killed the Lynch Amendment,
and then he voted for Dodd-Frank.

1269
01:12:12.911 --> 01:12:15.748
So, we need
to keep the facts straight here.

1270
01:12:16.582 --> 01:12:19.293
[Crowley] You know, you've spoken
about the fact that there are

1271
01:12:19.376 --> 01:12:21.712
you know, good Democrats
and bad Democrats, and...

1272
01:12:21.795 --> 01:12:24.131
I'm willing to say that maybe there are
some bad Democrats,

1273
01:12:24.214 --> 01:12:25.299
people who've let us down.

1274
01:12:25.841 --> 01:12:29.803
Hiram Monserrate is someone
who actually cut his girlfriend's face

1275
01:12:29.887 --> 01:12:31.722
with a broken piece of glass.

1276
01:12:31.805 --> 01:12:35.392
My question to you is,
why have you sought his support,

1277
01:12:35.476 --> 01:12:37.895
why did you go to his club
and speak at his club?

1278
01:12:38.187 --> 01:12:39.605
I'd like to know why you did that.

1279
01:12:40.356 --> 01:12:42.149
So, that's a lie.

1280
01:12:42.608 --> 01:12:45.361
I have not s--
I have not sought his support.

1281
01:12:45.444 --> 01:12:49.907
I think that that-- that history is--
is absolutely reprehensible,

1282
01:12:49.990 --> 01:12:52.159
and you say, "Why are you at his club?"

1283
01:12:52.242 --> 01:12:56.789
I was at the only Latino Democratic Club
in East Elmhurst and Corona.

1284
01:12:57.039 --> 01:12:58.248
- That's where I was.
- [applause]

1285
01:12:58.332 --> 01:13:01.168
There are teachers there,
there are cross-walk guards there,

1286
01:13:01.585 --> 01:13:04.505
and that was-- that is who
I was talking to that day.

1287
01:13:04.588 --> 01:13:08.842
So, while I know that women tend
to be made responsible

1288
01:13:08.926 --> 01:13:10.761
for the actions of every man in the room,

1289
01:13:10.844 --> 01:13:12.763
I am not. I am not.

1290
01:13:12.846 --> 01:13:14.181
[crowd cheering]

1291
01:13:15.933 --> 01:13:17.893
[Alexandria]
After ten years of failed leadership,

1292
01:13:17.976 --> 01:13:20.896
we have lost 1,000
Democratic seats nationwide.

1293
01:13:20.979 --> 01:13:23.273
We have lost the House,
we have lost the Senate,

1294
01:13:23.357 --> 01:13:24.733
we have lost the Presidency,

1295
01:13:24.817 --> 01:13:26.693
and it would be a profound mistake

1296
01:13:26.777 --> 01:13:30.197
if we believe that the same leadership
getting us into this mess

1297
01:13:30.280 --> 01:13:31.407
is gonna get us out.

1298
01:13:31.490 --> 01:13:34.993
Because what's gonna get us out
is improved and expanded Medicare for all.

1299
01:13:35.077 --> 01:13:37.788
What's gonna get us out
is a federal jobs guarantee.

1300
01:13:37.871 --> 01:13:40.541
What's gonna get us out
is the abolition of ICE.

1301
01:13:40.624 --> 01:13:43.919
What's gonna get us out
is a $15 minimum wage.

1302
01:13:44.002 --> 01:13:45.921
[cheering and applauding]

1303
01:13:46.004 --> 01:13:49.383
I think-- Miss Ocasio-Cortez,
I think you brought a lot of energy,

1304
01:13:49.466 --> 01:13:50.717
and I think you--

1305
01:13:51.093 --> 01:13:53.303
for someone who is not as experienced,

1306
01:13:53.387 --> 01:13:55.431
in terms of public life
and public service,

1307
01:13:55.514 --> 01:13:56.890
I think you're doing a great job
in that vein.

1308
01:13:56.974 --> 01:13:59.393
- Well, look, I do--
- [moderator] No outbursts, please!

1309
01:13:59.476 --> 01:14:01.812
I do thank her for the energy
she's brought to this.

1310
01:14:01.895 --> 01:14:05.524
What I'm hoping for is that that energy
doesn't end on Tuesday,

1311
01:14:05.607 --> 01:14:08.569
that that energy is brought forward
to November.

1312
01:14:08.819 --> 01:14:11.405
[crowd] Crowley! Crowley!

1313
01:14:11.488 --> 01:14:13.198
Hi, man, how are you? Nice to see you.

1314
01:14:13.282 --> 01:14:15.868
Crowley! Crowley!

1315
01:14:27.254 --> 01:14:29.631
- Hi, how are you?
- Hi, don't forget to vote.

1316
01:14:29.715 --> 01:14:32.509
Today's an election.
We vote right in that corner.

1317
01:14:32.593 --> 01:14:34.136
- All right, thank you.
- Thank you.

1318
01:14:36.096 --> 01:14:38.348
[man] Morning. It's election day.
Don't forget to vote.

1319
01:14:38.640 --> 01:14:40.309
- Vote for Alexandria.
- [Alexandria chuckles]

1320
01:14:41.059 --> 01:14:42.895
- Hi, good morning.
- Morning. Good luck today.

1321
01:14:42.978 --> 01:14:44.938
- Thank you!
- [man] Don't forget to vote.

1322
01:14:48.442 --> 01:14:50.736
Good morning.

1323
01:14:50.819 --> 01:14:53.322
- It's election day, don't forget to vote.
- Today's the election...

1324
01:14:53.405 --> 01:14:54.239
Thank you.

1325
01:14:57.576 --> 01:14:59.453
- [soft piano music playing]
- [Alexandria] Ready?

1326
01:14:59.870 --> 01:15:02.289
Let's go. Go.

1327
01:15:02.831 --> 01:15:03.999
Let's go.

1328
01:15:08.420 --> 01:15:11.465
- Hi! How are you, ladies?
- Very good.

1329
01:15:11.548 --> 01:15:13.091
- Thank you so much.
- Okay.

1330
01:15:13.175 --> 01:15:14.134
Nice to meet you.

1331
01:15:14.218 --> 01:15:16.261
- Your name, please.
- Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez.

1332
01:15:16.345 --> 01:15:17.763
You know that. [laughs]

1333
01:15:30.275 --> 01:15:31.485
[Alexandria] Thank you!

1334
01:15:31.902 --> 01:15:33.195
Bye-bye, all.

1335
01:15:33.529 --> 01:15:35.447
- Thank you so much.
- Good luck.

1336
01:15:35.531 --> 01:15:37.574
- I saw the video clip but I saw--
- Oh, amazing!

1337
01:15:37.658 --> 01:15:38.867
I didn't see it all through,

1338
01:15:38.951 --> 01:15:40.994
but the first two minutes
convinced me to come here.

1339
01:15:41.078 --> 01:15:43.372
Oh, my gosh. Thank you so much.

1340
01:16:08.981 --> 01:16:12.025
[Alexandria]
This district is 85% Democrat.

1341
01:16:13.360 --> 01:16:15.696
So, whoever wins the Primary,

1342
01:16:15.779 --> 01:16:17.948
essentially wins the General Election.

1343
01:16:21.702 --> 01:16:24.329
There hasn't been
any public polling in the race.

1344
01:16:26.206 --> 01:16:30.544
When we finally got enough money
to do a poll at the last minute,

1345
01:16:30.627 --> 01:16:33.505
it showed us about 35 points behind.

1346
01:16:33.797 --> 01:16:35.173
[woman]
You know where your poll is.

1347
01:16:35.257 --> 01:16:37.551
So, here's some information
about our candidate.

1348
01:16:37.634 --> 01:16:38.844
You got it! You got it!

1349
01:16:39.553 --> 01:16:43.307
[Alexandria] I get scared of the cynicism

1350
01:16:43.974 --> 01:16:45.934
that could result

1351
01:16:46.018 --> 01:16:49.021
from people really believing
in something

1352
01:16:49.104 --> 01:16:50.856
and then it not working out.

1353
01:16:51.940 --> 01:16:53.609
- You guys voting today?
- Thank you.

1354
01:16:53.692 --> 01:16:55.611
Vote for Alex.

1355
01:16:55.694 --> 01:16:57.154
Oh... Vote for Alex.

1356
01:16:57.237 --> 01:16:59.156
[man] Polls are open for 90 more minutes.

1357
01:17:01.033 --> 01:17:02.576
I just don't want to let them down.

1358
01:17:12.169 --> 01:17:13.837
[Alexandria] I don't wanna hear anything.

1359
01:17:15.172 --> 01:17:16.340
[phone beeps]

1360
01:17:16.923 --> 01:17:18.884
Mami, can you put your phone on mute?

1361
01:17:18.967 --> 01:17:20.218
[Blanca] Okay. Mm-hmm.

1362
01:17:21.887 --> 01:17:23.305
Oh, my God! Oh, my God!

1363
01:17:23.388 --> 01:17:26.016
- What?
- Oh, my God, no. I just...

1364
01:17:26.099 --> 01:17:27.434
[Blanca] Calm down.

1365
01:17:27.768 --> 01:17:30.270
Sorry. No, sorry. It's nothing.

1366
01:17:31.813 --> 01:17:35.442
[whispers] It's not nothing.
I saw press running to our party.

1367
01:17:36.860 --> 01:17:37.944
[indistinct chatter]

1368
01:17:39.863 --> 01:17:40.947
[Alexandria sighs]

1369
01:17:43.367 --> 01:17:44.534
- I love you.
- I love you, too.

1370
01:17:44.618 --> 01:17:47.079
I'm so proud of you.
Whatever happens, okay?

1371
01:17:47.162 --> 01:17:48.955
- We did the best we could do. Okay?
- We did.

1372
01:17:49.039 --> 01:17:51.166
- You did good.
- You did great. We did great.

1373
01:17:54.628 --> 01:17:57.422
No. I'm scared.
I don't know if I want to go in.

1374
01:17:58.006 --> 01:17:59.174
I'm scared.

1375
01:17:59.257 --> 01:18:01.051
- Should we walk in right now?
- Yeah, it's fine.

1376
01:18:01.134 --> 01:18:02.636
- You think so?
- Let's do it.

1377
01:18:07.307 --> 01:18:09.434
Oh, my God! Oh, my God!

1378
01:18:09.518 --> 01:18:11.019
Wait, wait, guys. Wait...

1379
01:18:11.103 --> 01:18:12.729
- [man1] Oh, my God!
- It's me! That's me!

1380
01:18:12.813 --> 01:18:15.273
- That's me on the poster! I'm sorry...
- [man 2] Uh... Oh, hey!

1381
01:18:15.357 --> 01:18:17.984
Hey! I'm sorry! Oh, my God!

1382
01:18:18.068 --> 01:18:19.319
[all screaming with excitement]

1383
01:18:21.988 --> 01:18:22.989
What is it?

1384
01:18:23.115 --> 01:18:24.157
I can't let--

1385
01:18:25.492 --> 01:18:27.911
on television right now.

1386
01:18:27.994 --> 01:18:29.287
How are you feeling?

1387
01:18:29.663 --> 01:18:31.540
- Can you put it into words?
- No.

1388
01:18:31.623 --> 01:18:32.624
[all laughing]

1389
01:18:32.708 --> 01:18:34.626
- I cannot put this into words.
- All right.

1390
01:18:34.710 --> 01:18:37.796
Your supporters here
are very excited for you!

1391
01:18:37.879 --> 01:18:39.464
This was a grassroots campaign.

1392
01:18:39.548 --> 01:18:41.550
- It absolutely was.
- Can you believe the numbers?

1393
01:18:41.633 --> 01:18:44.803
I cannot believe these numbers right now.

1394
01:18:44.886 --> 01:18:48.014
But I do know
that every single person here

1395
01:18:48.098 --> 01:18:52.436
has worked their butt off to change
the future of the Bronx and Queens.

1396
01:18:52.519 --> 01:18:53.729
That's what I know.

1397
01:18:53.812 --> 01:18:55.939
[reporter] When you said
that you were gonna take on Joe Crowley,

1398
01:18:56.022 --> 01:18:57.983
people looked at you like you were crazy.

1399
01:18:58.066 --> 01:18:59.317
They did. They did.

1400
01:18:59.401 --> 01:19:01.611
- And maybe I was a little bit.
- [all laughing]

1401
01:19:01.695 --> 01:19:04.990
I mean, you're going up against
the Queens Machine, as they call it.

1402
01:19:05.073 --> 01:19:07.367
Yeah, yeah. But, you know what?

1403
01:19:07.451 --> 01:19:09.619
We need a machine with a movement

1404
01:19:09.703 --> 01:19:11.371
and that is what we have done today.

1405
01:19:11.455 --> 01:19:13.874
Well, go ahead and celebrate
with your supporters.

1406
01:19:13.957 --> 01:19:15.250
[all screaming with excitement]

1407
01:19:18.462 --> 01:19:20.380
- Eighty-two percent of vote!
- Yes!

1408
01:19:20.464 --> 01:19:21.590
Oh, my God!

1409
01:19:21.965 --> 01:19:23.258
Congratulations!

1410
01:19:23.341 --> 01:19:24.801
God, thank you!

1411
01:19:24.885 --> 01:19:26.261
[man] Alexandria the Great!

1412
01:19:26.344 --> 01:19:27.387
[screaming with excitement]

1413
01:19:34.394 --> 01:19:37.355
[Corbit] Do you want any calls
with senators and such that keep calling?

1414
01:19:37.439 --> 01:19:39.232
The ones that didn't endorse you
along the way?

1415
01:19:39.316 --> 01:19:41.860
Or you wanna talk to them tomorrow?
It's up to you, obviously.

1416
01:19:41.943 --> 01:19:43.653
- Let's talk tomorrow.
- Okay.

1417
01:19:43.737 --> 01:19:44.613
All right.

1418
01:19:44.696 --> 01:19:46.990
It's my first time meeting you.
I live in the Bronx--

1419
01:19:47.073 --> 01:19:49.075
[all screaming with excitement]

1420
01:20:27.489 --> 01:20:28.532
[footsteps]

1421
01:20:35.038 --> 01:20:37.666
- [man] I love you!
- Oh, thank you!

1422
01:20:44.548 --> 01:20:45.382
[Riley chuckles]

1423
01:20:53.473 --> 01:20:54.307
[Alexandria sighs]

1424
01:21:14.828 --> 01:21:16.329
[Alexandria] When I was a little girl,

1425
01:21:17.163 --> 01:21:20.417
my Dad wanted to go on a road trip
with his buddies.

1426
01:21:21.710 --> 01:21:25.922
I wanted to go so badly,
and I begged and I begged,

1427
01:21:26.006 --> 01:21:27.841
and he relented.

1428
01:21:28.258 --> 01:21:29.342
And so...

1429
01:21:30.677 --> 01:21:34.431
it was like four grown men
and a five-year-old girl

1430
01:21:34.806 --> 01:21:37.225
went on this road trip from New York.

1431
01:21:39.352 --> 01:21:42.022
We stopped... We stopped here.

1432
01:21:44.649 --> 01:21:46.651
And it was a really beautiful day,

1433
01:21:47.819 --> 01:21:51.031
and he leaned down next to me, and he--

1434
01:21:51.114 --> 01:21:52.991
he pointed at the Washington Monument,

1435
01:21:53.074 --> 01:21:54.868
and he pointed at the Reflecting Pool,

1436
01:21:54.951 --> 01:21:57.287
and he pointed at everything, and he said,

1437
01:21:58.288 --> 01:21:59.915
he said, "You know,

1438
01:21:59.998 --> 01:22:01.833
this all belongs to us."

1439
01:22:02.125 --> 01:22:02.959
[sniffles]

1440
01:22:03.877 --> 01:22:04.711
[sniffles]

1441
01:22:05.587 --> 01:22:08.590
He said, "This is our government.
It belongs to us.

1442
01:22:08.673 --> 01:22:10.717
So all of this stuff is yours."

1443
01:22:18.016 --> 01:22:22.354
The last thing my Dad ever told me
was to make him proud.

1444
01:22:25.899 --> 01:22:26.733
[sighs]

1445
